
    const papersData = [{"id": "MCA37505", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA37505", "title": "Data Science", "type": "Core Major (Integrated Theory + Lab)", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA37505_Data_Science.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "Which of the following imputation techniques preserves the covariance structure between multivariate features better than mean substitution?", "options": ["Mode Imputation", "Multiple Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE)", "Arbitrary Constant Imputation", "Zero Padding"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "MICE models each variable with missing values conditioned upon the other variables, preserving joint distribution and covariance."}, {"q": "In principal component analysis (PCA), what do the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix represent?", "options": ["The variance explained by each feature", "The directions of maximal variance in the feature space", "The cluster centroids in reduced dimension", "The regression coefficients of target variable"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Eigenvectors determine the principal axes or directions of maximal variance, while eigenvalues quantify the variance magnitude."}, {"q": "What is the primary vulnerability of the Pearson correlation coefficient when assessing relationships between variables?", "options": ["It assumes non-linear monotonic dependencies", "It is sensitive to bivariate outliers and only captures linear dependencies", "It cannot evaluate continuous variables", "It is bounded strictly between 0 and 1"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Pearson coefficient is strictly linear and heavily distorted by outliers; Spearman or Kendall is preferred for monotonic non-linear relations."}, {"q": "In hypothesis testing, a Type II error occurs when:", "options": ["The null hypothesis is rejected when it is actually true", "The null hypothesis is failed to be rejected when it is actually false", "Both null and alternative hypotheses are accepted", "The p-value exceeds the alpha threshold when null is true"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Type II error (beta) is false negative: failing to reject a false null hypothesis."}, {"q": "Which dimensionality reduction algorithm non-linearly maps high-dimensional data by preserving local probabilistic neighborhood structures using Student-t distribution in low-dimensional space?", "options": ["Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)", "Principal Component Analysis (PCA)", "t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE)", "Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "t-SNE uses Student-t distribution with 1 degree of freedom in the low-dimensional map to alleviate the crowding problem."}, {"q": "In Pandas, which method allows memory-efficient lazy evaluation of grouping aggregations without instantiating intermediate DataFrames?", "options": ["df.groupby(..., observed=False)", "df.applymap()", "df.groupby(as_index=False).agg()", "df.to_parquet()"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Direct aggregation on groupby with specified columnar dictionary executes optimized vectorized aggregations in Cython."}, {"q": "Which evaluation metric is mathematically insusceptible to class imbalance when evaluating binary classification threshold performance?", "options": ["Accuracy Score", "Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (PR-AUC)", "Micro-averaged F1 Score", "Logarithmic Loss without weights"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "PR-AUC focuses on positive class precision and recall without incorporating true negatives, rendering it sensitive to minority class dynamics."}, {"q": "In a box-and-whisker plot, an observation is considered an extreme outlier if it lies outside:", "options": ["[Q1 - 0.5*IQR, Q3 + 0.5*IQR]", "[Q1 - 1.5*IQR, Q3 + 1.5*IQR]", "[Q1 - 3.0*IQR, Q3 + 3.0*IQR]", "[Mean - 2*StdDev, Mean + 2*StdDev]"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Mild outliers lie between 1.5 and 3.0 IQR; extreme outliers lie beyond 3.0 IQR from the first and third quartiles."}, {"q": "What condition must be satisfied for Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression estimators to be BLUE as per the Gauss-Markov theorem?", "options": ["Errors must follow a Poisson distribution", "Errors must have homoscedasticity and zero conditional mean", "Features must exhibit strict multicollinearity", "Number of features must exceed observation sample size"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Gauss-Markov states that OLS is Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) if errors have zero conditional mean, homoscedasticity, and no autocorrelation."}, {"q": "Which distance metric is best suited for high-dimensional sparse text vectors generated from TF-IDF?", "options": ["Euclidean Distance", "Manhattan Distance", "Cosine Similarity", "Minkowski Distance (p=3)"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Cosine similarity measures angular orientation irrespective of vector magnitude, making it immune to document length variations."}, {"q": "In scikit-learn, what is the role of `ColumnTransformer` in a production machine learning pipeline?", "options": ["To tune hyperparameters across GPUs", "To apply distinct preprocessing transformers to different column subsets in parallel", "To automatically balance imbalanced target labels", "To convert tabular data directly into CNN tensors"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "ColumnTransformer enables heterogeneous feature preprocessing (e.g. One-Hot Encoding for categorical, StandardScaling for numeric)."}, {"q": "Which sampling technique generates synthetic examples along feature-space line segments connecting k-nearest neighbors of the minority class?", "options": ["Random Under-Sampling", "Tomek Links", "SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique)", "NearMiss"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "SMOTE interpolates synthetic feature vectors along line segments connecting minority instances with their k-nearest neighbors."}, {"q": "What does a high Variance Inflation Factor (VIF > 10) signify in multiple linear regression?", "options": ["Severe residual heteroscedasticity", "Severe multicollinearity among independent variables", "Non-normality of dependent variable", "Overfitting due to excessive polynomials"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "VIF measures how much variance of an estimated regression coefficient increases due to collinearity; VIF > 5 or 10 denotes critical multicollinearity."}, {"q": "Which statistical test is used to assess independence between two categorical variables in a contingency table?", "options": ["Paired t-test", "Pearson Chi-Square Test of Independence", "One-Way ANOVA", "Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Chi-Square test checks whether observed cell frequencies in a contingency table deviate significantly from expected frequencies under independence."}, {"q": "In A/B testing, what is the 'p-value' formally defined as?", "options": ["The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true", "The probability of observing results at least as extreme as those observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true", "The probability of committing a Type I error in 100 iterations", "The ratio of treatment group conversions to control group conversions"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "P-value is the probability under the null hypothesis of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the actual observed value."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Distinguish between Parametric and Non-parametric statistical models with suitable examples.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Define fixed vs flexible parameter space. Parametric assumes underlying distribution (e.g., Linear Regression, Naive Bayes); Non-parametric does not (e.g., KNN, Decision Trees, Kernel Density Estimation)."}, {"q": "Explain the mathematical formulation and significance of Z-score normalization vs Min-Max scaling.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Z-score: (x - mu)/sigma yielding mu=0, sigma=1 (robust to boundaries, sensitive to outliers). Min-Max: (x - min)/(max - min) bounding values in [0, 1]."}, {"q": "Define Central Limit Theorem (CLT). Why is it foundational to inferential statistics in data science?", "marks": 3, "hint": "States distribution of sample means approaches normal distribution as sample size n >= 30, regardless of the population distribution shape."}, {"q": "What is multicollinearity? How does Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) detect it?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Correlation among regressors; VIF_i = 1 / (1 - R_i^2). Explain how VIF > 5-10 indicates regression variance inflation and unstable beta coefficients."}, {"q": "Illustrate the Trade-off between Precision and Recall. In which practical scenario is Recall prioritized over Precision?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Inverse relationship when shifting decision threshold. High recall is critical in cancer diagnosis, fraud/terror detection where False Negatives carry catastrophic cost."}, {"q": "Describe the mathematical procedure of Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) for continuous probability estimation.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Explain non-parametric smoothing formula f_hat(x) = (1/nh) sum K((x - x_i)/h) where K is kernel (e.g. Gaussian) and h is bandwidth parameter."}, {"q": "Contrast Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) with Confirmatory Data Analysis (CDA).", "marks": 3, "hint": "EDA explores data patterns without preconceived hypotheses (using visualizations, box plots, scatter matrices); CDA formally tests pre-formulated hypotheses using statistical significance tests."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Derive the mathematical formulation of Principal Component Analysis (PCA). Explain step-by-step how the covariance matrix, eigenvalues, and eigenvectors are computed to achieve optimal projection.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Step 1: Mean centering X. Step 2: Sample covariance Sigma = (1/n) X^T X. Step 3: Solve characteristic equation det(Sigma - lambda*I) = 0. Step 4: Sort eigenvalues and select top k eigenvectors as projection matrix W. Step 5: Transform Y = X W."}, {"q": "Explain the complete Data Wrangling and Feature Engineering pipeline for a raw tabular dataset containing missing values, categorical features of high cardinality, and extreme outliers.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Detailed workflow: 1. MCAR/MAR missing analysis (MICE/IterativeImputer). 2. Outlier treatment (Winsorization/IQR capping). 3. Encoding: Target Encoding/Frequency Encoding for high cardinality. 4. Skewness correction: Box-Cox/Yeo-Johnson log transforms."}, {"q": "A clinical trial tests a new drug against a placebo across two patient cohorts (n1=100, n2=100). Detail the formulation of Null & Alternative Hypotheses, formulation of the Two-Sample t-test statistic, degrees of freedom, decision rule using p-value vs alpha=0.05, and confidence intervals.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define H0: mu1 = mu2, H1: mu1 != mu2. State t-statistic formula with pooled sample variance s_p^2, df = n1 + n2 - 2. Clarify critical region and p-value evaluation."}, {"q": "Explain the architecture of a production-grade scikit-learn Pipeline incorporating FeatureUnion or ColumnTransformer, Hyperparameter Grid Search (GridSearchCV), and Cross-Validation (K-Fold vs Stratified K-Fold).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Draw/describe architecture: Raw Data -> ColumnTransformer (Numeric: SimpleImputer+StandardScaler; Categorical: OneHotEncoder) -> Estimator. Explain why Stratified K-Fold prevents data leakage during hyperparameter tuning."}, {"q": "Compare and contrast ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, Cohen's Kappa, and Logarithmic Loss. Which metric would you select for a credit card fraud detection system with a 0.05% positive rate? Justify mathematically.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Explain that ROC-AUC gives overly optimistic results because False Positive Rate stays small with huge True Negatives. PR-AUC focuses directly on Precision and Recall, revealing true performance on rare positive instances."}, {"q": "Discuss the mathematical mechanisms of unsupervised clustering algorithms: K-Means vs DBSCAN. Analyze their performance regarding cluster geometries (spherical vs arbitrary shapes), sensitivity to noise/outliers, and computational complexity.", "marks": 5, "hint": "K-Means: optimizes inertia (sum of squared Euclidean distances), fails on non-convex shapes, O(n*k*i). DBSCAN: density connectivity using eps and MinPts, identifies noise (-1), handles arbitrary shapes, O(n log n) with spatial index."}, {"q": "Explain the bias-variance decomposition of Mean Squared Error in supervised learning models. Demonstrate how Ridge (L2) and Lasso (L1) regularization mathematically control variance and induce sparsity.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Decomposition: MSE = Bias^2 + Variance + Irreducible Error. Ridge adds lambda*sum(beta_j^2) shrinking weights smoothly. Lasso adds lambda*sum(|beta_j|), diamond contour intersections at axes drive non-essential coefficients to exact zero."}, {"q": "Demonstrate how to architect an end-to-end Time Series forecasting analysis using Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA). Explain stationarity tests (Augmented Dickey-Fuller) and order estimation using ACF and PACF plots.", "marks": 5, "hint": "ADF test to determine order of differencing d until p-value < 0.05. PACF cutoff determines AR order p; ACF cutoff determines MA order q. Formulate AR(p) + I(d) + MA(q) equation."}]}, {"id": "MCA30506", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA30506", "title": "Natural Language Processing", "type": "Core Major Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA30506_Natural_Language_Processing.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "What is the primary difference between Stemming (e.g. Porter Stemmer) and Lemmatization (WordNet)?", "options": ["Stemming uses contextual embeddings; lemmatization does not", "Stemming applies heuristic rule-based affix stripping; lemmatization uses morphological analysis with a vocabulary lexicon", "Stemming always produces a valid dictionary root word", "Lemmatization can only be performed on uppercase text"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Stemming chops word ends heuristically (e.g., 'caring' -> 'car'), whereas lemmatization resolves genuine lemma using vocabulary and POS tags."}, {"q": "In the Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) subword tokenization algorithm, how are new vocabulary tokens iteratively created?", "options": ["By splitting words at punctuation boundaries", "By iteratively merging the most frequent adjacent pair of characters/tokens", "By calculating the maximum entropy of syllables", "By computing TF-IDF scores across words"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "BPE starts with character vocabulary and iteratively merges the most frequent co-occurring symbol pairs into new tokens."}, {"q": "Which smoothing technique in N-gram language modeling redistributes probability mass by using absolute discounting combined with lower-order N-gram continuation probability?", "options": ["Laplace (Add-1) Smoothing", "Good-Turing Smoothing", "Kneser-Ney Smoothing", "Witten-Bell Smoothing"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Interpolated Kneser-Ney uses absolute discounting and models how likely a lower-order word is to complete an unseen context (continuation probability)."}, {"q": "What is the computational complexity of finding the optimal hidden state sequence using the Viterbi Algorithm in an HMM with N states and observation length T?", "options": ["O(N * T)", "O(N^2 * T)", "O(N^T)", "O(T^N)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Viterbi maintains a dynamic programming trellis where each of the T steps evaluates transitions between all N states, resulting in O(N^2 * T)."}, {"q": "In the Continuous Bag of Words (CBOW) Word2Vec architecture, what is the training objective?", "options": ["Predict context words given a single target word", "Predict target word given surrounding context words", "Predict the sentence sentiment label", "Maximize cross-entropy over character sequences"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "CBOW predicts the current target word given context words, whereas Skip-Gram predicts context words from the target word."}, {"q": "Which mathematical operation in the Transformer self-attention mechanism prevents softmax gradients from vanishing into regions with extremely small gradients?", "options": ["Multiplying by temperature tau > 10", "Scaling the dot products by 1 / sqrt(d_k)", "Applying Layer Normalization after feedforward", "Adding sinusoidal positional encodings"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Dividing Q*K^T by sqrt(d_k) prevents the dot products from growing excessively large for high dimensions, keeping softmax gradients stable."}, {"q": "What fundamental limitation of recurrent neural networks (RNNs) does the Transformer architecture directly resolve?", "options": ["Inability to generate text in real-time", "Sequential processing bottleneck preventing parallelization during training", "Excessive reliance on self-attention", "High latency during embedding lookup"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Transformers replace recurrence with self-attention, processing all tokens concurrently and enabling massive parallel training on GPUs."}, {"q": "BERT is trained using which pair of self-supervised objective functions?", "options": ["Autoregressive Next Token Prediction & Contrastive Loss", "Masked Language Modeling (MLM) & Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)", "Skip-Gram Loss & Negative Sampling", "Sequence-to-Sequence Teacher Forcing"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "BERT is an encoder pre-trained on Masked LM (15% masked tokens) and Next Sentence Prediction (binary classification of consecutive segments)."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of Positional Encodings in Transformer architectures?", "options": ["To compress embedding dimensions into integers", "To inject token sequence order information into the permutation-invariant self-attention mechanism", "To encode part-of-speech tags into embeddings", "To eliminate the need for softmax activation"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Self-attention is permutation-invariant (order-agnostic). Positional encodings (sinusoidal or learned) restore token sequence order."}, {"q": "In Named Entity Recognition (NER), why is a BiLSTM-CRF architecture superior to a standalone BiLSTM with softmax?", "options": ["CRF eliminates backward propagation", "CRF captures transition dependencies between consecutive output tags (e.g. I-PER cannot follow B-LOC)", "BiLSTM cannot handle token embeddings", "CRF computes self-attention across tokens"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A Conditional Random Field (CRF) layer models label transition probabilities, enforcing global constraints on tag sequence consistency."}, {"q": "Perplexity is a standard evaluation metric for language models. Lower perplexity corresponds to:", "options": ["Worse prediction capability", "Higher branching factor and lower model confidence", "Higher test set likelihood and superior model performance", "Overfitting to stop words"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Perplexity is reciprocal geometric average probability: PP(W) = P(W)^(-1/N). Lower perplexity indicates higher probability assigned to ground truth."}, {"q": "Which text representation algorithm combines matrix factorization of global word co-occurrence statistics with local context window features?", "options": ["Word2Vec Skip-Gram", "GloVe (Global Vectors)", "FastText", "One-Hot Representation"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "GloVe minimizes a log-bilinear model on non-zero elements of the global word co-occurrence matrix."}, {"q": "In machine translation evaluation, the BLEU metric primarily computes:", "options": ["Character-level Levenshtein edit distance", "Modified n-gram precision penalized by a brevity penalty", "Syntactic dependency tree similarity", "Recall against human synonym dictionaries"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "BLEU calculates geometric mean of modified n-gram precisions multiplied by an exponential brevity penalty for short candidate translations."}, {"q": "What distinguishes an Autoregressive model (like GPT) from an Autoencoding model (like BERT)?", "options": ["GPT uses bidirectional attention; BERT uses causal masking", "GPT predicts next token conditioned solely on left context; BERT reconstructs masked tokens using bidirectional context", "GPT has no decoder layers", "BERT cannot be fine-tuned"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "GPT is a causal (unidirectional) autoregressive model ideal for generation; BERT is a bidirectional autoencoder ideal for sequence classification/NER."}, {"q": "What is the role of the Softmax Temperature hyperparameter during text generation?", "options": ["Controls learning rate during backpropagation", "Smooths or sharpens the next-token probability distribution to control randomness/creativity", "Truncates token vocabulary size to top-k", "Normalizes embedding vectors to unit norm"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Dividing logits by temperature T < 1 sharpens distribution (deterministic); T > 1 flattens distribution (more diverse/random outputs)."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the concept of TF-IDF. Write its mathematical formula and explain why IDF penalizes ubiquitous words.", "marks": 3, "hint": "TF = f(t, d)/sum f(w, d). IDF = log(N / df(t)). Common words like 'the' appear in every document (df = N), leading to log(1) = 0."}, {"q": "Contrast Skip-Gram with Negative Sampling (SGNS) vs Hierarchical Softmax in Word2Vec training.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Full softmax over vocabulary V is O(|V|). SGNS converts multi-class prediction into binary logistic regressions for 1 true pair and k noise pairs. Hierarchical Softmax uses Huffman tree reducing complexity to O(log |V|)."}, {"q": "Explain the three fundamental problems of Hidden Markov Models (Evaluation, Decoding, Learning) and their algorithms.", "marks": 3, "hint": "1. Evaluation: Forward-Backward algorithm. 2. Decoding: Viterbi algorithm. 3. Learning: Baum-Welch (EM) algorithm."}, {"q": "What is the Out-Of-Vocabulary (OOV) problem in NLP? How do subword tokenization models (WordPiece/BPE) resolve it?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Fixed vocabularies map unseen words to <UNK>, losing meaning. Subword algorithms segment unseen words into familiar morphological sub-units (e.g. 'unaffable' -> 'un' + 'aff' + 'able')."}, {"q": "Describe the attention mechanism formulated by Bahdanau et al. (Additive Attention) for Seq2Seq architectures.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Computes alignment score e_ij = v_a^T tanh(W_a s_{i-1} + U_a h_j). Softmax produces attention weights alpha_ij; context vector c_i is dynamic weighted sum of encoder hidden states."}, {"q": "What is Dependency Parsing? How does it differ from Constituency (Phrase Structure) Parsing?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Constituency breaks sentences into nested syntactic phrases (NP, VP). Dependency parsing establishes direct typed binary grammatical links between head words and their dependents (nsubj, dobj, amod)."}, {"q": "Explain the role of Masked Multi-Head Attention in the Transformer Decoder during inference vs training.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Masks future positions with -infinity before softmax during training so position i cannot attend to position > i, preserving causality identical to step-by-step inference."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Derive the mathematical formulation of Scaled Dot-Product Attention and Multi-Head Attention in the Transformer. Explain the dimensions of matrices Q, K, V, and why multi-head attention is superior to single-head attention.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax((Q K^T) / sqrt(d_k)) V. MultiHead = Concat(head_1, ..., head_h) W^O where head_i = Attention(Q W_i^Q, K W_i^K, V W_i^V). Allows model to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions."}, {"q": "Explain the complete architectural framework of BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers). Detail its input representation (Token + Segment + Position Embeddings), Pre-training tasks (MLM & NSP), and Fine-tuning paradigms.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Input is sum of three embeddings. MLM masks 15% tokens (80% [MASK], 10% random, 10% unchanged). NSP predicts IsNext/NotNext. Fine-tuning adds simple task classification head over [CLS] token representation."}, {"q": "Explain the Viterbi algorithm for Part-of-Speech (POS) tagging using a Hidden Markov Model. Provide the recurrence relation, initialization, induction step, and back-pointer path reconstruction.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define trellis v_t(j) = max_i [v_{t-1}(i) * a_{ij}] * b_j(o_t). Backpointer bp_t(j) stores argmax. Show initialization at t=1, recursive computation for t=2..T, and traceback from max state at T."}, {"q": "Design an end-to-end Sentiment Analysis system using modern NLP pipelines. Explain data ingestion, text normalization, tokenization, contextual fine-tuning with a pre-trained Transformer, handling class imbalance, and production deployment via REST API.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Pipeline: Ingestion -> Cleaning (emojis, links) -> Hugging Face AutoTokenizer -> Pre-trained model (e.g. RoBERTa) -> CrossEntropy with class weights / focal loss -> Evaluation with Macro-F1 -> FastAPI serving."}, {"q": "Analyze the mechanics of parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods, specifically Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). How does LoRA freeze base model weights while updating low-rank decomposition matrices A and B? State memory and computational benefits.", "marks": 5, "hint": "W_new = W_0 + Delta W = W_0 + (alpha/r) * (B * A) where W_0 in R^{d x k}, B in R^{d x r}, A in R^{r x k} with r << min(d, k). Freezes W_0, drastically reducing trainable parameters by 99% while maintaining inference latency."}, {"q": "Compare Seq2Seq evaluation metrics: BLEU, ROUGE (ROUGE-N, ROUGE-L), and BERTScore. Explain how BERTScore uses contextual embeddings to overcome exact string matching limitations.", "marks": 5, "hint": "BLEU = n-gram precision with brevity penalty. ROUGE = n-gram recall and longest common subsequence. BERTScore computes cosine similarity between contextual BERT embeddings of candidate and reference tokens with greedy matching."}, {"q": "Discuss Text Summarization paradigms: Extractive vs Abstractive Summarization. Detail the Seq2Seq with Pointer-Generator Network and Coverage mechanism designed by See et al.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Extractive selects key sentences; Abstractive paraphrases novel sentences. Pointer-Generator combines generation from vocabulary with copying tokens from source via generation probability p_gen. Coverage mechanism eliminates repetition."}, {"q": "Detail the Word2Vec Skip-Gram model with Negative Sampling (SGNS). Write the complete objective loss function and explain how stochastic gradient ascent updates target and context word representations.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Objective: maximize log sigma(v'_{w_O}^T v_{w_I}) + sum_{i=1}^k E_{w_i ~ P_n(w)} [log sigma(-v'_{w_i}^T v_{w_I})]. Explain sigmoid probabilities and gradient derivatives with respect to target and context embeddings."}]}, {"id": "MCA30508", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA30508", "title": "Cloud Computing", "type": "Core Major Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA30508_Cloud_Computing.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "According to the NIST definition, which of the following is NOT one of the five essential characteristics of Cloud Computing?", "options": ["On-demand self-service", "Broad network access", "Autonomous quantum processing", "Measured service"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "NIST defines 5 characteristics: On-demand self-service, Broad network access, Resource pooling, Rapid elasticity, and Measured service."}, {"q": "Which cloud service model delegates OS administration, runtime management, and physical infrastructure to the CSP while allowing the consumer to deploy application code?", "options": ["Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)", "Platform as a Service (PaaS)", "Software as a Service (SaaS)", "Hardware as a Service (HaaS)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "In PaaS, consumers deploy application code and data; the CSP manages OS, runtime, virtualization, storage, and networking."}, {"q": "What is the core distinction between a Type-1 (Bare-Metal) and a Type-2 (Hosted) Hypervisor?", "options": ["Type-1 runs directly on hardware; Type-2 runs on top of a host operating system", "Type-1 supports only Windows; Type-2 supports Linux", "Type-1 does not support hardware virtualization extensions", "Type-2 cannot execute multiple guest VMs"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Type-1 (e.g. ESXi, KVM, Xen) runs directly on bare metal. Type-2 (e.g. VirtualBox, VMware Workstation) runs as software inside a host OS."}, {"q": "Which storage type is best suited for databases requiring sub-millisecond random Read/Write operations on an AWS EC2 instance?", "options": ["Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service)", "Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2)", "Amazon Glacier Deep Archive", "Amazon EFS with Infrequent Access"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "EBS provides persistent block storage directly attached over low-latency SAN, optimal for transactional databases."}, {"q": "In containerization, what Linux kernel primitives form the basis of process isolation and resource limitation respectively?", "options": ["systemd and crontab", "Namespaces and Control Groups (cgroups)", "SELinux and AppArmor", "iptables and IPVS"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Namespaces isolate process views (PID, NET, MNT, IPC, UTS); cgroups meter and throttle resource utilization (CPU, memory, I/O)."}, {"q": "What is the primary function of an AWS Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) NAT Gateway?", "options": ["To allow internet traffic to reach instances in private subnets directly", "To enable instances in a private subnet to connect outbound to the internet while preventing inbound connections", "To serve as a cryptographic hardware security module", "To balance HTTP/HTTPS traffic across multi-AZ instances"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A NAT Gateway allows outbound internet access for private subnet workloads (e.g. OS patches) while blocking unsolicited inbound internet traffic."}, {"q": "Under the AWS Shared Responsibility Model, which security task is the sole responsibility of the customer when running EC2?", "options": ["Physical security of server racks and data centers", "Hypervisor patch management", "Operating system updates, security patches, and firewall rules (Security Groups)", "Decommissioning defective magnetic hard drives"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "AWS manages security OF the cloud (infrastructure, hardware, hypervisors); customers manage security IN the cloud (OS patches, network configuration, IAM, application code)."}, {"q": "Which cloud computing design principle advocates that cloud resources should be provisioned automatically to match fluctuating workload demand?", "options": ["Statelessness", "Elasticity", "Multi-tenancy", "Vendor Lock-in"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources in or out dynamically according to real-time workload demand."}, {"q": "What defines a 'Serverless' compute service like AWS Lambda?", "options": ["No physical servers exist anywhere in the cloud architecture", "Developers do not manage or provision servers, execution is event-driven, and billing is based purely on execution duration and memory", "Compute jobs run continuously without timeout", "Applications cannot access external databases"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Serverless abstracts server management; execution is triggered by events, auto-scales instantly, and charges only for elapsed execution milliseconds."}, {"q": "What does the CAP theorem state regarding distributed data stores across network partitions?", "options": ["A distributed system can achieve Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance simultaneously", "When a network partition occurs, a system must trade off between Consistency and Availability", "Performance is inversely proportional to database capacity", "ACID transactions cannot be implemented on relational tables"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "CAP states that in the event of network partition (P), a distributed system can maintain Consistency (CP) or Availability (AP), but not both."}, {"q": "In Kubernetes architecture, which master plane component is responsible for maintaining the cluster's distributed key-value state store?", "options": ["kube-scheduler", "kube-apiserver", "etcd", "kube-controller-manager"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "etcd is the strongly consistent, distributed key-value store used as Kubernetes' backing store for all cluster data and state."}, {"q": "Which Cloud Deployment Model is provisioned for exclusive use by a single organization comprising multiple consumers (e.g., business units)?", "options": ["Public Cloud", "Private Cloud", "Community Cloud", "Federated Cloud"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Private Cloud is dedicated solely to a single enterprise, hosted either on-premises or by a third party."}, {"q": "In Service Level Agreements (SLAs), what does 'Four Nines' (99.99%) availability represent in terms of maximum allowable annual downtime?", "options": ["Approximately 8 hours 45 minutes", "Approximately 52 minutes 36 seconds", "Approximately 5 minutes 15 seconds", "Approximately 3 days 14 hours"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "99.99% availability translates to (1 - 0.9999) * 365.25 * 24 * 60 = 52.56 minutes of allowable downtime per year."}, {"q": "Which AWS networking component acts as a virtual distributed router connecting VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub?", "options": ["Internet Gateway", "AWS Direct Connect Hub", "AWS Transit Gateway", "Elastic Load Balancer"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "AWS Transit Gateway simplifies network topology by acting as a cloud router connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks."}, {"q": "What is the primary difference between Horizontal Scaling (Scaling Out/In) and Vertical Scaling (Scaling Up/Down)?", "options": ["Horizontal adds more CPU/RAM to an existing instance; Vertical adds more instances", "Horizontal adds or terminates instance nodes; Vertical upgrades or downgrades the hardware specifications of a single node", "Horizontal scaling causes downtime; vertical scaling never causes downtime", "Horizontal scaling only applies to relational databases"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Scale-out adds more nodes to distribute load; Scale-up replaces an existing server with one having higher CPU/RAM capacity."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the concept of Multi-Tenancy in cloud computing. How is logical tenant data isolation maintained?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Multi-tenancy shares single physical infrastructure among multiple clients. Logical isolation is achieved via tenant IDs, DB schema segregation, VPC network ACLs, and encryption with distinct KMS keys."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Hardware Virtualization (Hypervisors) and Operating System-level Virtualization (Containers).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Hypervisors virtualize underlying hardware; each VM has its own guest OS kernel (heavyweight, minutes boot). Containers share the host OS kernel using namespaces and cgroups (lightweight, milliseconds boot)."}, {"q": "Describe the core features of Object Storage (e.g., AWS S3). How does it differ fundamentally from Block Storage (EBS)?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Object storage treats data as discrete objects with metadata and unique identifiers accessed via HTTP REST APIs (unstructured, infinite scale). Block storage divides data into fixed sectors mounted as raw file system."}, {"q": "Explain the role and working mechanism of a Content Delivery Network (CDN) like Amazon CloudFront.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Globally distributed Edge Locations cache static and dynamic web content close to end users, reducing latency, TCP handshake time, and origin server load."}, {"q": "What is Cloud Disaster Recovery? Briefly define RTO (Recovery Time Objective) and RPO (Recovery Point Objective).", "marks": 3, "hint": "RTO: maximum acceptable duration of system outage before restoration. RPO: maximum acceptable data loss measured backwards in time from disaster event."}, {"q": "Explain the Shared Responsibility Model in cloud security across IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS.", "marks": 3, "hint": "IaaS: customer manages OS up to app; PaaS: customer manages data and app; SaaS: customer only manages user access/identity; provider manages everything below."}, {"q": "What are Microservices? Explain how they overcome the limitations of Monolithic architectures in cloud deployments.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Decomposes application into small, independent, loosely coupled services communicating via APIs. Enables independent scaling, modular deployments, fault isolation, and polyglot tech stacks."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Design a highly available, fault-tolerant, multi-tier web application architecture on AWS spanning two Availability Zones. Detail the integration of Route 53, Application Load Balancer, Auto Scaling Groups, Multi-AZ RDS, and S3.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Diagram/explain: DNS -> Route53 -> ALB across 2 Public Subnets -> Auto Scaling EC2 instances in 2 Private Subnets -> Aurora/RDS with synchronous standby replica in second AZ -> Static assets in S3 with CloudFront CDN."}, {"q": "Analyze the architecture of Kubernetes. Explain the function of Control Plane components (API Server, Controller Manager, Scheduler, etcd) and Worker Node components (kubelet, kube-proxy, Container Runtime).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Control Plane: kube-apiserver (REST gateway), etcd (consensus state), kube-scheduler (assigns pods to nodes), controller-manager (reconciles state loops). Worker node: kubelet (ensures pods run), kube-proxy (network rules/VIP), container runtime (Docker/containerd)."}, {"q": "Discuss Cloud Security mechanisms: IAM (Identity and Access Management), Principle of Least Privilege, Multi-Factor Authentication, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), and Data Encryption (At-Rest via AES-256 vs In-Transit via TLS 1.3).", "marks": 5, "hint": "IAM policies (JSON permissions), temporary role credentials (AWS STS), RBAC matrix. Encryption at rest via KMS customer master keys (envelope encryption) and in-transit using modern TLS cryptographic handshakes."}, {"q": "Explain the architectural principles of Serverless Computing. Analyze its cold-start latency, stateless execution model, event-driven triggers, and economic pricing model compared to provisioned VM instances.", "marks": 5, "hint": "No idle server costs; scale from zero to thousands. Cold start occurs when a new container instance initializes runtime/dependencies. Managed via Provisioned Concurrency, lightweight binaries, and externalized state (DynamoDB/ElastiCache)."}, {"q": "Evaluate Cloud Migration strategies as outlined in Gartner's 6R framework: Rehost, Replatform, Repurchase, Refactor, Retire, and Retain. Provide practical scenarios for each strategy.", "marks": 5, "hint": "1. Rehost (Lift and Shift). 2. Replatform (Lift, Tinker, Shift - e.g. switch to managed RDS). 3. Repurchase (Move to SaaS). 4. Refactor/Re-architect (Full cloud-native rewrite). 5. Retire (Decommission). 6. Retain (Keep on-prem legacy)."}, {"q": "Analyze Distributed Consensus algorithms in cloud systems. Explain the Paxos and Raft consensus algorithms and their critical role in maintaining leader election and log replication across distributed nodes.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Raft decomposes consensus into Leader Election, Log Replication, and Safety. Heartbeat timers, candidate state, vote requests, quorum calculation (N/2 + 1) to ensure single source of truth without split-brain."}, {"q": "Explain Infrastructure as Code (IaC) paradigms: Declarative vs Imperative. Compare Terraform with AWS CloudFormation, detailing state management, execution plans, and immutable infrastructure delivery.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Declarative (desired state defined in HCL/YAML) vs Imperative (procedural scripts). Terraform state file (terraform.tfstate) tracks real-world infrastructure; terraform plan previews deltas before apply; immutable updates replace rather than modify."}, {"q": "Compare NoSQL database categories deployed in the cloud: Key-Value (DynamoDB), Document (MongoDB), Column-Family (Cassandra), and Graph (Neo4j). Detail their partitioning strategies, consistency trade-offs, and target use cases.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Key-value: simple primary key lookup, ultra-low latency; Document: JSON nesting, flexible indexing; Column-family: high write throughput, wide row partitioning across cluster; Graph: relation traversal without expensive joins."}]}, {"id": "BCA30104", "programme": "Bachelor of Computer Applications (BCA) - 2026", "code": "BCA30104", "title": "Data Structure and Algorithm", "type": "Core Major Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "BCA30104_Data_Structure_and_Algorithm.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "What is the worst-case time complexity of searching for an element in an unsorted Singly Linked List of n nodes?", "options": ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n log n)"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "In an unsorted singly linked list, sequential traversal from the head node is required, which takes O(n) in the worst case."}, {"q": "Which data structure follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) discipline and is inherently utilized during recursive function execution?", "options": ["Queue", "Stack", "Binary Tree", "Circular Buffer"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Stacks operate on LIFO and form the system call stack managing return addresses and local activation records during recursion."}, {"q": "What is the result of evaluating the postfix expression: `6 3 2 + * 5 -`?", "options": ["25", "30", "20", "15"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Stack steps: Push 6, 3, 2. '+': pop 2, 3 -> 3+2=5. Push 5. '*': pop 5, 6 -> 6*5=30. Push 30, push 5. '-': pop 5, 30 -> 30-5=25."}, {"q": "In a Circular Queue of capacity N using an array, what is the condition indicating that the queue is completely full?", "options": ["front == rear", "front == (rear + 1) % N", "rear == N - 1", "(front + 1) % N == rear"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "In circular queue with one slot left empty to distinguish from full, full condition is (rear + 1) % N == front."}, {"q": "What is the maximum number of nodes in a binary tree of height h (where a tree with single root node has height 1)?", "options": ["2^h", "2^h - 1", "2^(h-1)", "2^(h+1) - 1"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A complete binary tree has sum_{i=0}^{h-1} 2^i = 2^h - 1 nodes."}, {"q": "Which traversal of a Binary Search Tree (BST) visits nodes in strictly ascending sorted order?", "options": ["Preorder Traversal", "Inorder Traversal", "Postorder Traversal", "Level-order Traversal"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Inorder traversal visits Left Subtree -> Root -> Right Subtree, which yields ascending keys in any valid BST."}, {"q": "What is the balance factor of a node in an AVL tree defined as?", "options": ["Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree)", "Number of Left Children - Number of Right Children", "Depth of Node / Degree of Node", "Height of Tree - Depth of Node"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Balance factor BF = height(left) - height(right), and must remain in {-1, 0, +1} for all nodes in an AVL tree."}, {"q": "Which sorting algorithm exhibits the best worst-case time complexity among the following?", "options": ["Bubble Sort", "Quick Sort", "Merge Sort", "Insertion Sort"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Merge Sort guarantees O(n log n) in all cases (best, average, and worst), whereas Quick Sort, Bubble Sort, and Insertion Sort can degrade to O(n^2)."}, {"q": "In Quick Sort, which partitioning choice avoids the O(n^2) worst-case on already sorted arrays?", "options": ["Always selecting the first element", "Always selecting the last element", "Randomized pivot selection or Median-of-Three", "Selecting the minimum element"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Selecting a random pivot or median-of-three prevents degenerate splits on already sorted or reverse-sorted inputs."}, {"q": "What is the minimum number of queues required to implement a Stack data structure efficiently?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Two FIFO queues are required to simulate a LIFO stack by transferring elements back and forth during push or pop operations."}, {"q": "In hashing with open addressing, what is the probe sequence formula for Linear Probing?", "options": ["h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) % m", "h(k, i) = (h'(k) + c1*i + c2*i^2) % m", "h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i*h2(k)) % m", "h(k, i) = (h'(k) * i) % m"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Linear probing checks consecutive slots sequentially: h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) mod m for i = 0, 1, 2, ... m-1."}, {"q": "Which graph algorithm determines the Single-Source Shortest Path in a weighted directed graph with non-negative edge weights?", "options": ["Prim's Algorithm", "Kruskal's Algorithm", "Dijkstra's Algorithm", "Floyd-Warshall Algorithm"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Dijkstra's algorithm finds single-source shortest paths on non-negative weighted graphs in O((V + E) log V) with min-priority queue."}, {"q": "A connected undirected graph with V vertices is a tree if and only if it contains exactly how many edges?", "options": ["V edges", "V - 1 edges", "V + 1 edges", "V * (V - 1) / 2 edges"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "By graph theory definition, a tree on V vertices is an acyclic connected graph having exactly V - 1 edges."}, {"q": "Which data structure is utilized in Breadth-First Search (BFS) traversal of a graph?", "options": ["Stack", "Queue", "Priority Queue", "Binary Search Tree"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "BFS visits vertices level-by-level using a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) queue."}, {"q": "What is the time complexity of deleting a node from a doubly linked list when a direct pointer to the target node is provided?", "options": ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n^2)"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Because each node has direct pointers to both previous and next nodes, unlinking can be done in O(1) without list traversal."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the advantages and disadvantages of Linked Lists over static Arrays.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Arrays: contiguous memory, O(1) random access, fixed size, expensive insertion/deletion O(n). Linked Lists: dynamic sizing, efficient O(1) insertion/deletion with pointer, no random access O(n), extra pointer memory overhead."}, {"q": "Write an algorithm or C function to reverse a Singly Linked List in place in O(n) time.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Use three pointers (prev=NULL, curr=head, next=NULL). In loop: next = curr->next; curr->next = prev; prev = curr; curr = next; finally head = prev."}, {"q": "Explain the concept of Circular Queue. How does it eliminate the memory waste of linear array queues?", "marks": 3, "hint": "In linear queue, once rear reaches capacity, vacant front slots cannot be reused. Circular queue wraps indices using modulo arithmetic: rear = (rear + 1) % MAX."}, {"q": "Convert the following Infix expression to Postfix notation using a stack: `(A + B) * (C - D) / E`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Show operator stack and output string at each step. Final postfix expression: `A B + C D - * E /`."}, {"q": "Define an AVL Tree. Explain the Balance Factor and why height balancing is crucial for search efficiency.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Self-balancing BST where |height(left) - height(right)| <= 1 for all nodes. Ensures tree height remains bounded by O(log n), preventing worst-case O(n) skew."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Breadth First Search (BFS) and Depth First Search (DFS) graph traversals.", "marks": 3, "hint": "BFS: uses Queue, explores level-by-level, finds shortest unweighted path. DFS: uses Stack/Recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking."}, {"q": "What is collision in Hashing? Briefly explain Separate Chaining vs Open Addressing.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Collision occurs when two distinct keys map to the same bucket index h(k1) == h(k2). Separate chaining stores colliding keys in a linked list; open addressing probes alternative empty array slots."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Write complete C / Java code or detailed algorithm to implement a Double-Ended Queue (Deque) using a circular array with operations: insertFront, insertRear, deleteFront, deleteRear.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Detail data structure with front, rear, size, capacity. Write index arithmetic using modulo for wrapping and boundary conditions (empty and full checks)."}, {"q": "Construct an AVL Tree by inserting the following sequence of keys into an initially empty tree: `50, 20, 60, 10, 8, 15, 30, 25`. Show the tree structure and specify rotations (LL, RR, LR, RL) performed at each step.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Step-by-step tree drawings: insert 50, 20, 60 (balanced). Insert 10, 8 -> LL rotation at 20. Insert 15 -> LR rotation at 20. Insert 30, 25 -> RL rotation at 50/30. Compute BF after every insertion."}, {"q": "Explain the Quick Sort algorithm. Trace the execution of Quick Sort on the array `[38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10]` using Lomuto or Hoare partitioning. Analyze its Best, Average, and Worst-case time complexities.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Trace pivot selection, partitioning, and recursive subarrays. Best/Avg: O(n log n) with balanced partitions. Worst: O(n^2) when pivot is always smallest/largest element."}, {"q": "Explain Dijkstra's Algorithm for Single Source Shortest Path. Apply it to find the shortest path from Source vertex A to all other vertices in a given weighted directed graph. Provide the distance table update trace.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define graph G=(V,E). Initialize dist[s]=0, dist[v]=inf. Maintain visited set. Select unvisited node with min distance, relax edges: if dist[u] + w(u,v) < dist[v] then dist[v] = dist[u] + w. Show step-by-step table."}, {"q": "Explain Minimum Spanning Tree (MST). Formulate Kruskal's Algorithm using Disjoint Set Union (DSU with Union-by-Rank and Path Compression). Find the MST for a 6-vertex weighted graph.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Sort edges in ascending weight. Add edges greedily if they do not create cycles using Find-Set(u) != Find-Set(v). Total edges in MST must be V - 1. Time complexity: O(E log E)."}, {"q": "Write algorithms for the three standard traversals of a Binary Tree: Inorder, Preorder, and Postorder. For a given tree with Preorder: `A B D E C F` and Inorder: `D B E A F C`, reconstruct the unique Binary Tree.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Provide recursive traversal algorithms. Reconstruction: Preorder first element A is root. Locate A in Inorder: left subtree is `D B E`, right subtree is `F C`. Repeat recursively for subtrees."}, {"q": "Explain the concept of Heap Data Structure. Differentiate between Max-Heap and Min-Heap. Explain the Heapify operation and show step-by-step how the array `[12, 11, 13, 5, 6, 7]` is converted into a Max-Heap.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Complete binary tree satisfying heap-order property. Show array representation where left=2i+1, right=2i+2. Start heapify from last non-leaf node index n/2 - 1 downwards. Show swaps resulting in max-heap."}, {"q": "Compare Hashing Collision Resolution techniques: Separate Chaining, Linear Probing, Quadratic Probing, and Double Hashing. Discuss their behavior regarding primary clustering, secondary clustering, and load factor threshold alpha.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Linear probing suffers from primary clustering (contiguous blocks). Quadratic probing resolves primary but has secondary clustering (same hash yields same probe sequence). Double hashing eliminates both using independent step size h2(k). Separate chaining handles alpha > 1 gracefully."}]}, {"id": "BCA30203", "programme": "Bachelor of Computer Applications (BCA) - 2026", "code": "BCA30203", "title": "Artificial Intelligence", "type": "Core Minor Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "BCA30203_Artificial_Intelligence.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "What is the Turing Test designed to evaluate?", "options": ["A computer's algorithmic sorting speed", "Whether a machine can exhibit human-indistinguishable intelligent behavior", "The computational complexity of NP-complete problems", "The physical robotics agility of an autonomous agent"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Alan Turing proposed the imitation game to test whether an interrogator can distinguish between human and machine responses."}, {"q": "Which type of intelligent agent maintains internal state to track aspects of the environment that cannot be viewed currently?", "options": ["Simple Reflex Agent", "Model-based Reflex Agent", "Goal-based Agent without state", "Passive Random Agent"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A Model-based agent keeps track of internal state representing unobserved parts of the environment."}, {"q": "What makes an environment 'Deterministic' from an agent's perspective?", "options": ["The next state is completely determined by current state and agent's action", "The environment contains other competing intelligent agents", "The agent cannot perceive its own sensory data", "The state space is continuous and infinite"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "An environment is deterministic if the next state is completely determined by the current state and the executed action."}, {"q": "Which uninformed search strategy is guaranteed to find the shallowest goal state first and is complete for finite branching factors?", "options": ["Depth-First Search (DFS)", "Breadth-First Search (BFS)", "Depth-Limited Search (DLS)", "Bidirectional DFS"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "BFS systematically expands the search frontier level by level, ensuring the shallowest goal is found first."}, {"q": "In heuristic search, what condition must a heuristic h(n) satisfy to be classified as 'Admissible' in A* search?", "options": ["h(n) must always equal the true cost h*(n)", "h(n) must never overestimate the true cost to reach the nearest goal state", "h(n) must be strictly greater than the path cost g(n)", "h(n) must be non-monotonic"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "An admissible heuristic never overestimates: 0 <= h(n) <= h*(n) for all nodes n, guaranteeing A* optimality on tree search."}, {"q": "What problem occurs in Hill Climbing search when the algorithm reaches a flat area where all neighbouring states have the same evaluation value?", "options": ["Ridge", "Plateau", "Global Maximum", "Local Minimum"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A plateau is a flat region of the state-space landscape where all neighbouring states have identical heuristic values, halting greedy progress."}, {"q": "In the Minimax algorithm with Alpha-Beta pruning, what does the parameter Beta represent?", "options": ["The best value that the Maximizer can guarantee", "The best (lowest-cost) value that the Minimizer can guarantee so far along the path", "The depth limit of the game tree", "The branching factor of the root node"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Alpha is the highest-value choice found so far for MAX; Beta is the lowest-value choice found so far for MIN."}, {"q": "Which inference rule allows deriving proposition Q given `P -> Q` and `P`?", "options": ["Modus Tollens", "Modus Ponens", "Resolution", "Conjunction Introduction"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Modus Ponens: From P and P -> Q, we infer Q."}, {"q": "In First-Order Logic (FOL), how is the statement 'Every student loves computers' formally written?", "options": ["exists x (Student(x) ^ Loves(x, Computers))", "forall x (Student(x) -> Loves(x, Computers))", "forall x (Student(x) ^ Loves(x, Computers))", "exists x (Student(x) -> Loves(x, Computers))"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Universal quantifier forall x naturally pairs with implication (->) to assert properties over all members of a class."}, {"q": "What is the process of converting First-Order Logic formulas into Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF) where existential variables are replaced with functions called?", "options": ["Unification", "Skolemization", "Resolution Refutation", "Modus Tollens"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Skolemization eliminates existential quantifiers by replacing existential variables with Skolem constants or Skolem functions of universally quantified variables."}, {"q": "In a Rule-based Expert System, what component executes the rules against facts stored in the working memory?", "options": ["Knowledge Base", "Inference Engine", "Explanation Facility", "Knowledge Acquisition Module"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The inference engine applies inference rules (forward or backward chaining) to deduced facts in the working memory."}, {"q": "Forward chaining in deductive knowledge systems is characterized as being:", "options": ["Goal-driven", "Data-driven", "Hypothesis-driven", "Backtracking-only"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Forward chaining starts with known facts and applies rules forward to extract new conclusions (data-driven)."}, {"q": "What is the main limitation of a single-layer perceptron identified by Minsky and Papert (1969)?", "options": ["Inability to compute continuous values", "Inability to learn linearly non-separable functions such as the XOR logic gate", "Excessive number of training weights", "Lack of activation threshold"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A single-layer perceptron can only form linear decision boundaries and cannot classify linearly non-separable problems like XOR."}, {"q": "Which search algorithm uses iterative deepening to combine the space efficiency of DFS with the completeness of BFS?", "options": ["A* Search", "Iterative Deepening Search (IDS)", "Greedy Best-First Search", "Uniform Cost Search (UCS)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "IDS runs depth-limited search with increasing depth bounds (d = 0, 1, 2, ...), requiring only O(bd) memory while remaining complete."}, {"q": "In Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP), what is the term for eliminating values from a variable domain that violate constraints with neighbor variables?", "options": ["Forward Checking and Arc Consistency (AC-3)", "Skolemization", "Minimax Pruning", "Genetic Mutation"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Arc Consistency algorithms (like AC-3) iteratively prune domain values that have no valid consistent assignment in neighbouring variables."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the PEAS (Performance measure, Environment, Actuators, Sensors) framework with an example of an Automated Taxi Driver.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Performance: safety, speed, legal, comfort, profit. Environment: roads, traffic, pedestrians, weather. Actuators: steering, accelerator, brake, horn. Sensors: cameras, LIDAR, speedometer, GPS."}, {"q": "Compare Informed (Heuristic) Search with Uninformed (Blind) Search strategies.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Uninformed (BFS, DFS) has no domain guidance beyond problem formulation; explores blind. Informed (A*, Greedy Best-First) uses heuristic function h(n) estimating distance to goal to guide search efficiently."}, {"q": "Explain the working principle of the Hill Climbing search algorithm and its failure modes (Local maxima, Ridges, Plateaus).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Greedy local search moving in direction of increasing value. Fails at: Local Maxima (peaks lower than global), Ridges (sequence of local maxima), Plateau (flat zero-gradient region)."}, {"q": "What is Alpha-Beta Pruning in adversarial game trees? Write the pruning condition.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Optimization of Minimax eliminating branches that cannot influence the final decision. Alpha = best max value; Beta = best min value. Pruning condition: if alpha >= beta, prune the remaining sub-branch."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Forward Chaining and Backward Chaining in Knowledge-based systems.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Forward Chaining: Data-driven, starts from premises/facts and infers consequences. Backward Chaining: Goal-driven, starts with hypothesis and searches backwards for supporting premises."}, {"q": "Explain the Unification algorithm in First Order Logic with a suitable example.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Finding a substitution theta that makes two distinct First-Order Logic atomic sentences identical: UNIFY(Knows(John, x), Knows(John, Jane)) yields theta = {x/Jane}."}, {"q": "Describe the architecture and primary components of an Expert System.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Components: Knowledge Base (rules/facts), Inference Engine (reasoning algorithms), Working Memory (current state), User Interface, Explanation Facility."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Explain the A* Search algorithm in detail. Prove that A* search is optimal when the heuristic function h(n) is admissible on tree search. Analyze its time and space complexity.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Evaluation function f(n) = g(n) + h(n). Prove that if a suboptimal goal G2 is popped from priority queue, f(G2) = g(G2) > g(G*) >= f(n), contradicting priority queue min extraction. Memory complexity O(b^d) remains its main bottleneck."}, {"q": "Trace the Minimax algorithm with Alpha-Beta pruning on a two-player zero-sum game tree of depth 3 with given terminal node payoffs. Indicate explicitly which branches are pruned and calculate the final minimax value at the root.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Draw standard 8-leaf game tree (e.g. leaves: 3, 5, 6, 9, 1, 2, 0, -1). Propagate alpha and beta bounds down and backup min/max values. Demonstrate exact points where alpha >= beta triggers cutoff."}, {"q": "Explain Resolution Refutation in Propositional and First Order Logic. Convert the following premises into CNF and prove by resolution that 'John is happy':<br/>1. Anyone who passes exams and does not fail is happy.<br/>2. John passed exams.<br/>3. John did not fail.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Negate goal: ~Happy(John). Convert premises to CNF clauses: Clause 1: ~Pass(x) v Fail(x) v Happy(x); Clause 2: Pass(John); Clause 3: ~Fail(John); Clause 4: ~Happy(John). Resolve clauses sequentially to derive empty clause (contradiction)."}, {"q": "Formulate the 8-Queens Problem as a Constraint Satisfaction Problem (CSP). Detail the variables, domains, and non-attacking constraints. Explain how Backtracking search combined with Forward Checking solves it.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Variables: Q1..Q8 (rows). Domain for each: {1..8} (columns). Constraints: Qi != Qj (no same column), |Qi - Qj| != |i - j| (no diagonal attack). Explain how forward checking prunes remaining row domains immediately upon placing Queen i."}, {"q": "Discuss Knowledge Representation schemes in AI: Semantic Networks, Frames, Conceptual Dependency, and First-Order Predicate Logic. Highlight their strengths and representational expressiveness.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Semantic Nets: graphical nodes (concepts) and labeled directed arcs (is-a, has-a). Frames: object-oriented slots and default fillers with inheritance. CD: Schank's primitive actions (TRANS, ATRANS, PTRANS). FOL: formal mathematical semantics and sound inference rules."}, {"q": "Explain the mathematical model of an Artificial Neuron (Perceptron). Describe the Perceptron Learning Algorithm and discuss why multi-layer networks with non-linear activation functions (Sigmoid/ReLU) are required for non-linear decision boundaries.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Model: y = f(w^T x + b). Weight update rule: w_i := w_i + eta * (y - y_hat) * x_i. Explain why linear combinations cannot solve XOR. Non-linear activation enables Universal Approximation across stacked hidden layers."}, {"q": "Detail the architecture and search strategy of Genetic Algorithms (GAs). Explain the biological analogy, chromosome encoding, fitness function, selection mechanisms (Roulette Wheel vs Tournament), Crossover operators, and Mutation.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Population-based metaheuristic. Chromosome = candidate solution bit-string. Fitness function assigns survival score. Crossover combines parental substrings. Mutation introduces random bit flips to escape local optima. Generational loop continues until convergence."}, {"q": "Explain Natural Language Processing pipeline stages in AI systems: Morphological Analysis, Syntactic Parsing, Semantic Interpretation, and Pragmatic Analysis. Discuss common ambiguities encountered in each phase.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Breakdown: 1. Morphology (word stems, affixes). 2. Syntax (grammar rules, parse trees). 3. Semantics (literal meaning, compositional semantics). 4. Pragmatics (contextual intent, discourse). Give examples of lexical, syntactic (e.g. PP attachment), and referential ambiguity."}]}, {"id": "BCA30001", "programme": "Bachelor of Computer Applications (BCA) - 2026", "code": "BCA30001", "title": "Vedic Mathematics", "type": "Interdisciplinary Course (IC) Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "BCA30001_Vedic_Mathematics.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "How many main Sutras and Sub-sutras (Upa-sutras) are there in Vedic Mathematics as reconstructed by Swami Bharati Krishna Tirtha?", "options": ["12 Sutras and 10 Upa-sutras", "16 Sutras and 13 Upa-sutras", "18 Sutras and 15 Upa-sutras", "24 Sutras and 18 Upa-sutras"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Vedic Mathematics is based on 16 foundational Sutras and 13 Corollaries / Sub-sutras (Upa-sutras)."}, {"q": "What is the English translation of the foundational Vedic Sutra 'Ekadhikena Purvena'?", "options": ["All from 9 and the last from 10", "By one more than the previous one", "Vertically and crosswise", "When the sum is the same, it is zero"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "'Ekadhikena Purvena' literally means 'By one more than the previous one'."}, {"q": "Which Sutra is specifically utilized for multiplying two numbers that are close to a common base (e.g., 97 \u00d7 94 near base 100)?", "options": ["Nikhilam Navatashcaramam Dashatah", "Urdhva-Tiryakbhyam", "Paravartya Yojayet", "Shunyam Samyasamuccaye"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Nikhilam Navatashcaramam Dashatah' translates to 'All from 9 and the last from 10', used for base multiplication and division."}, {"q": "Using 'Ekadhikena Purvena', what is the square of 85?", "options": ["7025", "7225", "7425", "7625"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "For numbers ending in 5: Left part = 8 * (8 + 1) = 8 * 9 = 72; Right part = 5^2 = 25. Result = 7225."}, {"q": "What is the meaning and application of the Sutra 'Urdhva-Tiryakbhyam'?", "options": ["Vertically and Crosswise \u2014 General multiplication applicable to any numbers", "By addition and subtraction \u2014 Linear equations only", "Transpose and apply \u2014 Synthetic division", "Proportionately \u2014 Working base conversion"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Urdhva-Tiryakbhyam' means 'Vertically and Crosswise', which is the universal Vedic algorithm for polynomial and number multiplication."}, {"q": "What is the 'Beejank' (Digital Root) of the number 84,729?", "options": ["3", "4", "30", "9"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Sum of digits: 8 + 4 + 7 + 2 + 9 = 30 -> 3 + 0 = 3 (casting out nines: 9, 7+2, leaving 8+4=12 -> 3)."}, {"q": "Which Sutra translates to 'Transpose and Apply' and is widely used for algebraic division and solving linear equations?", "options": ["Paravartya Yojayet", "Anurupyena", "Sopantyadvayamantyam", "Yavadunam"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Paravartya Yojayet' means 'Transpose and Apply', used for synthetic division and algebraic root finding."}, {"q": "What is the result of multiplying 998 \u00d7 994 using the Nikhilam method?", "options": ["992012", "994012", "992006", "991012"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Base 1000. Deficiencies: -002, -006. Left part: 998 - 006 = 992. Right part: (-002) * (-006) = 012. Combine -> 992012."}, {"q": "The Sub-sutra 'Anurupyena' is applied when:", "options": ["Numbers are far from powers of 10 but close to a working base (sub-base like 50, 200, 500)", "Dividing by numbers ending with zero only", "Computing digital roots of fractions", "Factorizing cubic polynomials only"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Anurupyena' means 'Proportionately', used with working sub-bases (e.g. 50 = 100/2, 200 = 100*2)."}, {"q": "What does the Sutra 'Shunyam Samyasamuccaye' state regarding equations?", "options": ["When the collection/sum is the same on both sides, that collection is equal to zero", "Zero multiplied by any number is infinity", "All digits sum to nine", "Transposed coefficients sum to unity"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Shunyam Samyasamuccaye' means 'When the collection/sum is the same, it is equated to zero'."}, {"q": "Which Vedic algorithm is utilized for fast non-restoring straight division where numbers are partitioned using a flag digit?", "options": ["Dhvajanka (Flag Method)", "Duplex Method (Dwandwa Yoga)", "Vilokanam", "Gunakasamuccayah"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Dhvajanka ('On the Flag') is the universal straight division method using flag digits to compute quotients and remainders in single-line steps."}, {"q": "What is the 'Duplex' (Dwandwa Yoga) of the two-digit number 47?", "options": ["28", "56", "33", "65"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Duplex of a 2-digit number (ab) is 2 * a * b = 2 * 4 * 7 = 56."}, {"q": "Using the Sutra 'Ekanyunena Purvena', what is the result of multiplying 46 \u00d7 99?", "options": ["4554", "4545", "4654", "4556"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Left part: 46 - 1 = 45. Right part: 99 - 45 = 54. Concatenate: 4554."}, {"q": "What is the Duplex of a single digit number 'a'?", "options": ["a", "2 * a", "a^2", "a / 2"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "The Duplex of a single-digit number is its square: D(a) = a^2."}, {"q": "Which Vedic method uses visual observation without written calculation to solve simple algebraic structures?", "options": ["Vilokanam", "Chalana-Kalanabhyam", "Vyashthisamashthih", "Lopana Sthapanabhyam"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "'Vilokanam' means 'By mere observation / inspection'."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the concept of 'Beejank' (Digital Root). How is it used to verify arithmetic operations (addition and multiplication)?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Digital root is single digit obtained by summing digits repeatedly. Beejank(A * B) must equal Beejank(Beejank(A) * Beejank(B)). Demonstrate with example."}, {"q": "State and explain the Sutra 'Ekadhikena Purvena'. Demonstrate how it squares any number ending in 5.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Means 'By one more than the previous'. For (N5)^2 = [N * (N+1)] | [25]. Compute (75)^2 = 7*8 | 25 = 5625."}, {"q": "Multiply 94 by 89 using the Sutra 'Nikhilam Navatashcaramam Dashatah' showing deviations from base 100.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Base 100. 94 has deficiency -06, 89 has deficiency -11. Left part: 94 - 11 = 83. Right part: (-06) * (-11) = 66. Result: 8366."}, {"q": "Explain the 'Duplex' (Dwandwa Yoga) method. Compute the Duplex values for single-digit, two-digit, and three-digit numbers.", "marks": 3, "hint": "D(a) = a^2. D(ab) = 2ab. D(abc) = 2ac + b^2. Provide numerical examples: D(7)=49, D(34)=2*3*4=24, D(235)=2*2*5 + 3^2 = 29."}, {"q": "Solve the algebraic equation using 'Paravartya Yojayet': `(x + 1)(x + 2) = (x - 3)(x - 4)`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Expand or apply Vedic rule: x^2 + 3x + 2 = x^2 - 7x + 12 -> 10x = 10 -> x = 1. Explain the transposition principle."}, {"q": "Explain the working principle of the Sub-sutra 'Anurupyena' when numbers are close to 50 instead of 100.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Working base = 50 = 100 / 2. Compute deviations from 50. Left part must be divided by 2 before appending the right part product."}, {"q": "Describe the Sutra 'Shunyam Samyasamuccaye' and demonstrate its application in solving `1/(x+2) + 1/(x+3) = 1/(x+1) + 1/(x+4)`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Sum of denominator roots on LHS = (x+2)+(x+3) = 2x+5. RHS = (x+1)+(x+4) = 2x+5. Since sums are equal, equate 2x + 5 = 0 -> x = -5/2."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Explain the general multiplication Sutra 'Urdhva-Tiryakbhyam' (Vertically and Crosswise). Multiply `432 \u00d7 315` step-by-step showing all 5 crosswise steps, carry forwarding, and the final product.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Show 5 steps: 1) 2*5=10 (0, carry 1). 2) (3*5)+(2*1)+1=18 (8, carry 1). 3) (4*5)+(2*3)+(3*1)+1=30 (0, carry 3). 4) (4*1)+(3*3)+3=16 (6, carry 1). 5) (4*3)+1=13. Result: 136,080."}, {"q": "Explain the Vedic method of Squaring using the Duplex (Dwandwa Yoga) method. Compute the square of the 4-digit number `3425` showing step-by-step duplex operations and carry handling.", "marks": 5, "hint": "For number abcd, write sequence of duplexes: D(a), D(ab), D(abc), D(abcd), D(bcd), D(cd), D(d). Compute D(3), D(34), D(342), D(3425), D(425), D(25), D(5). Propagate carries leftwards to yield 11,730,625."}, {"q": "Explain the Dhvajanka (Straight Division) algorithm. Divide `74,385 by 83` using the Flag Method. Detail the flag digit, modified dividend, partial quotients, and final remainder.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Divisor 83: 8 is operating divisor, 3 is flag (placed above). Set up columns. Step-by-step computation: divide by 8, subtract (flag * previous quotient digit) from intermediate dividend. Yield quotient and remainder."}, {"q": "Demonstrate the calculation of Square Roots of non-perfect numbers using the Vedic formula based on Duplex. Find the square root of `5428` correct up to 3 decimal places.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Group digits into pairs: 54 and 28. Nearest square to 54 is 7^2=49 (remainder 5). Operating divisor = 2 * 7 = 14. Continue using duplex subtractions to generate successive decimal digits."}, {"q": "Explain the Sutra 'Nikhilam Navatashcaramam Dashatah' for Division when the divisor is just below a power of 10. Divide `12314 by 998` using the Nikhilam synthetic division scheme.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Base 1000. Divisor 998, complement = 002. Partition dividend into quotient (12) and remainder (314) slots (3 digits for base 1000). Multiply quotient digits by complement and add downwards to obtain Q=12, R=338."}, {"q": "Explain how Vedic Mathematics is applied to solve systems of Simultaneous Linear Equations in two variables using 'Paravartya Yojayet' or 'Anurupyena'. Solve:<br/>`3x + 4y = 18`<br/>`2x + 5y = 19` without standard elimination steps.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Use cross-multiplication determinant structure: x = (c1 b2 - c2 b1) / (a1 b2 - a2 b1), y = (a1 c2 - a2 c1) / (a1 b2 - a2 b1). x = (18*5 - 19*4)/(3*5 - 2*4) = (90-76)/(15-8) = 14/7 = 2. y = (3*19 - 2*18)/7 = (57-36)/7 = 21/7 = 3."}, {"q": "Explain the factorization of quadratic and cubic polynomials using the Vedic Sutra 'Lopana-Sthapanabhyam' (Elimination and Retention). Factorize: `2x^2 + 5xy + 2y^2 + 7x + 5y + 3`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Put y=0 -> factorize 2x^2 + 7x + 3 = (2x + 1)(x + 3). Put x=0 -> factorize 2y^2 + 5y + 3 = (2y + 3)(y + 1). Combine consistent factors: (2x + y + 1)(x + 2y + 3) or verify cross product terms."}, {"q": "Discuss the computational significance of Vedic Mathematics algorithms in modern Computer Science, specifically in Digital Signal Processing (DSP), High-Speed VLSI Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) design, and Low-Power Microprocessor multipliers.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Standard multipliers (Booth, Array) have propagation delay O(N^2) or complex carry trees. Urdhva-Tiryakbhyam generates partial products concurrently with independent carry propagation, drastically reducing gate delay and power in FPGA/ASIC DSP chips."}]}, {"id": "BIL30001", "programme": "BCA in Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning - 2026", "code": "BIL30001", "title": "Probability and Statistics", "type": "Interdisciplinary Course (IC) Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "BIL30001_Probability_and_Statistics.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "If events A and B are statistically independent, which of the following statements is strictly TRUE?", "options": ["P(A union B) = P(A) + P(B)", "P(A intersection B) = P(A) * P(B)", "P(A | B) = 0", "P(A) + P(B) = 1"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "By definition of statistical independence, joint probability is the product of marginals: P(A cap B) = P(A) * P(B)."}, {"q": "In Bayes' Theorem, what is P(B | A) called in the expression `P(A | B) = [P(B | A) * P(A)] / P(B)`?", "options": ["Prior Probability", "Posterior Probability", "Likelihood", "Evidence"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "P(A) is Prior, P(B|A) is Likelihood, P(B) is Evidence / Marginal Likelihood, and P(A|B) is Posterior."}, {"q": "What is the variance of a Binomial distribution with parameters n and p?", "options": ["n * p", "n * p * (1 - p)", "sqrt(n * p)", "n * (1 - p)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "For X ~ Binomial(n, p), Mean = n*p and Variance = n*p*(1-p) = n*p*q."}, {"q": "In a Poisson distribution, if the mean is lambda = 4, what is its standard deviation?", "options": ["4", "2", "16", "8"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "In a Poisson distribution, Variance equals Mean = lambda = 4. Hence Standard Deviation = sqrt(4) = 2."}, {"q": "What proportion of observations in a standard normal distribution Z ~ N(0, 1) lie within the interval [-1.96, +1.96]?", "options": ["68.27%", "90.00%", "95.00%", "99.73%"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "In a standard normal distribution, exactly 95% of probability mass lies between Z = -1.96 and Z = +1.96."}, {"q": "Which property states that `E[aX + bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y]` for any random variables X and Y?", "options": ["Markov Inequality", "Linearity of Expectation", "Law of Total Variance", "Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Linearity of expectation holds unconditionally for any random variables, whether dependent or independent."}, {"q": "What is the value of the Pearson Correlation Coefficient `r` if two variables have a perfect negative linear relationship?", "options": ["0", "-1", "+1", "-infinity"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Pearson's r is bounded in [-1, +1]; r = -1 indicates a deterministic negative linear slope."}, {"q": "In linear regression `Y = beta_0 + beta_1 * X`, what is the relationship between the slope `beta_1` and correlation `r`?", "options": ["beta_1 = r * (sigma_x / sigma_y)", "beta_1 = r * (sigma_y / sigma_x)", "beta_1 = r^2", "beta_1 = r / (sigma_x * sigma_y)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The regression slope beta_1 = Cov(X, Y) / Var(X) = r * (sigma_y / sigma_x)."}, {"q": "Which probability distribution models the time elapsed between consecutive Poisson events in a memoryless process?", "options": ["Geometric Distribution", "Exponential Distribution", "Gamma Distribution", "Weibull Distribution"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The continuous Exponential distribution models waiting time between Poisson arrivals and possesses the memoryless property."}, {"q": "In statistical hypothesis testing, the significance level alpha is the probability of committing a:", "options": ["Type I Error (False Positive)", "Type II Error (False Negative)", "Sampling Error", "Standard Error of the Mean"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Alpha = P(Reject H0 | H0 is True), which is the probability of a Type I error."}, {"q": "When is a Student's t-test used instead of a Z-test for testing sample means?", "options": ["When population variance is known and n > 100", "When population variance is unknown and sample size n is small (n < 30)", "When data is nominal and categorical", "When computing correlation matrices"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Student's t-test is used when the population standard deviation sigma is unknown and estimated using sample standard deviation s on small samples (n < 30)."}, {"q": "What is the sum of probabilities across the entire sample space for any discrete probability mass function (PMF)?", "options": ["0", "0.5", "1.0", "Depends on sample size"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "By Kolmogorov's second axiom, the total probability of the entire sample space sum P(X = x) = 1.0."}, {"q": "What does a Chi-Square goodness-of-fit test evaluate?", "options": ["Whether population variances are equal", "Whether sample data conforms to a specific theoretical distribution", "The slope of multiple linear regression", "Difference between two paired means"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Chi-Square goodness-of-fit tests whether observed sample frequencies differ significantly from expected theoretical frequencies."}, {"q": "In ANOVA (Analysis of Variance), what does the F-statistic measure?", "options": ["Ratio of Between-Group Variance to Within-Group Variance", "Ratio of Sample Mean to Population Median", "Sum of squared residual errors", "Difference between two proportions"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "F = MS_between / MS_within (Mean Square Between groups divided by Mean Square Within groups)."}, {"q": "What is the formula for the Moment Generating Function (MGF) of a continuous random variable X?", "options": ["M_X(t) = E[e^(tX)]", "M_X(t) = E[t^X]", "M_X(t) = E[X^t]", "M_X(t) = integral e^(-tx) dx"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "M_X(t) is defined as the expected value of e^(tX): integral_{-inf}^{+inf} e^(tx) f(x) dx."}], "group_b": [{"q": "State and prove Bayes' Theorem for a partition of sample space {B1, B2, ..., Bn}.", "marks": 3, "hint": "State total probability law P(A) = sum P(A|Bi)P(Bi). Substitute into conditional probability definition P(Bk|A) = P(A cap Bk) / P(A) to derive standard Bayes theorem."}, {"q": "Define Probability Mass Function (PMF) and Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) for a discrete random variable.", "marks": 3, "hint": "PMF p(x) = P(X = x) with p(x) >= 0 and sum p(x) = 1. CDF F(x) = P(X <= x) is monotonic non-decreasing with F(-inf)=0 and F(+inf)=1."}, {"q": "A machine produces components of which 5% are defective. If a random sample of 6 components is inspected, find the probability that exactly 2 are defective.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Binomial: n=6, p=0.05, q=0.95, k=2. P(X=2) = C(6,2) * (0.05)^2 * (0.95)^4 = 15 * 0.0025 * 0.8145 = 0.0305 (approx 3.05%)."}, {"q": "Explain the memoryless property of the Exponential distribution. Write its mathematical expression.", "marks": 3, "hint": "P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t) for all s, t >= 0. The future probability of an event does not depend on elapsed time."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Correlation and Regression. What does a correlation coefficient of r = 0 indicate?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Correlation measures strength and direction of linear association; regression models functional cause-and-effect dependency. r = 0 indicates no linear relationship (non-linear relations may still exist)."}, {"q": "Explain Type I and Type II errors in hypothesis testing. How are they related to power of a test?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Type I (alpha): rejecting true H0. Type II (beta): failing to reject false H0. Power of test = 1 - beta (probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis)."}, {"q": "Describe the properties of the Normal Distribution curve (Symmetry, Mean=Median=Mode, Area, Points of Inflection).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Bell-shaped, symmetric about x = mu, mean=median=mode. Total area under curve = 1. Points of inflection at mu - sigma and mu + sigma. 68-95-99.7 empirical rule."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Derive the Mean and Variance of a Poisson Distribution with parameter lambda starting from its probability mass function.", "marks": 5, "hint": "PMF: P(X=k) = (e^-lambda * lambda^k) / k!. E[X] = sum k * P(X=k) = lambda * e^-lambda * sum lambda^(k-1)/(k-1)! = lambda. Compute E[X(X-1)] = lambda^2. Var(X) = E[X(X-1)] + E[X] - (E[X])^2 = lambda^2 + lambda - lambda^2 = lambda."}, {"q": "In a factory producing semiconductor microchips, three machines A, B, and C produce 30%, 45%, and 25% of the total output. The defective rates are 2%, 3%, and 1% respectively. If a chip selected at random is found to be defective, calculate the probability that it was produced by Machine B using Bayes' Theorem.", "marks": 5, "hint": "P(A)=0.30, P(B)=0.45, P(C)=0.25. P(D|A)=0.02, P(D|B)=0.03, P(D|C)=0.01. Total P(D) = (0.30*0.02) + (0.45*0.03) + (0.25*0.01) = 0.006 + 0.0135 + 0.0025 = 0.022. P(B|D) = (0.45 * 0.03) / 0.022 = 0.0135 / 0.022 = 0.6136 (61.36%)."}, {"q": "Explain the Method of Least Squares for fitting a simple linear regression line `Y = a + bX`. Derive the normal equations for parameters `a` and `b`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define sum of squared residuals S = sum (y_i - a - b x_i)^2. Differentiate partially with respect to 'a' and set to 0 -> sum y = n*a + b*sum x. Differentiate with respect to 'b' and set to 0 -> sum xy = a*sum x + b*sum x^2. Solve for a and b."}, {"q": "Given the bivariate data:<br/>X: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5<br/>Y: 2, 5, 3, 8, 7<br/>Calculate the Pearson Correlation Coefficient `r` and determine the equation of the regression line of Y on X.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Calculate n=5, sum X=15, sum Y=25, mean X=3, mean Y=5. sum X^2=55, sum Y^2=151, sum XY=91. S_xx = 55 - (15^2)/5 = 10. S_yy = 151 - (25^2)/5 = 26. S_xy = 91 - (15*25)/5 = 16. r = 16 / sqrt(10 * 26) = 16 / 16.12 = 0.992. Slope b = 16/10 = 1.6. Intercept a = 5 - 1.6*3 = 0.2. Line: Y = 0.2 + 1.6X."}, {"q": "Explain the Central Limit Theorem (CLT). A random sample of size n = 64 is drawn from a population with mean mu = 50 and standard deviation sigma = 16. Find the probability that the sample mean lies between 48 and 52.", "marks": 5, "hint": "By CLT, X_bar ~ N(mu, sigma^2/n). Standard error SE = sigma / sqrt(n) = 16 / sqrt(64) = 2. Z1 = (48 - 50) / 2 = -1.0. Z2 = (52 - 50) / 2 = +1.0. P(-1 <= Z <= +1) = 2 * Phi(1.0) - 1 = 2 * (0.8413) - 1 = 0.6826 (68.26%)."}, {"q": "Explain the procedure of a Two-Sample Independent t-test for comparing two population means. State the assumptions, pooled variance formula, degrees of freedom, and decision rule.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Assumptions: normality, independence, homoscedasticity (equal variance). Pooled variance s_p^2 = [ (n1-1)s1^2 + (n2-1)s2^2 ] / (n1+n2-2). t-statistic = (x_bar1 - x_bar2) / [ s_p * sqrt(1/n1 + 1/n2) ] with df = n1 + n2 - 2. Reject H0 if |t| > t_critical."}, {"q": "Explain the Chi-Square Test of Independence for a 2x2 contingency table. A clinical trial yields: Treatment Success = 60, Failure = 40; Control Success = 40, Failure = 60. Test at alpha = 0.05 whether treatment success is independent of the group.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Compute row/column totals: Total n=200. Expected frequencies E_ij = (Row Total * Col Total) / Grand Total. E11=50, E12=50, E21=50, E22=50. Chi^2 = sum (O - E)^2 / E = (10^2)/50 + (-10^2)/50 + (-10^2)/50 + (10^2)/50 = 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.0. For df=(2-1)(2-1)=1, critical value at 0.05 is 3.841. Since 8.0 > 3.841, reject H0 (treatment has significant effect)."}, {"q": "Discuss One-Way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). Detail the partitioning of Total Sum of Squares (SST) into Between-Group (SSB) and Within-Group (SSW) Sum of Squares, degrees of freedom, and construction of the ANOVA Summary Table.", "marks": 5, "hint": "SST = SSB + SSW. Formulate SSB = sum n_i (x_bar_i - x_double_bar)^2 with df = k - 1. SSW = sum sum (x_ij - x_bar_i)^2 with df = N - k. Mean squares MSB = SSB / (k - 1), MSW = SSW / (N - k). F = MSB / MSW. Detail standard ANOVA table format."}]}, {"id": "BCA37002", "programme": "BCA & BCA (AI & ML) - Semester III - 2026", "code": "BCA37002 / BIL37002", "title": "Internet and Web Technologies", "type": "Skill Enhancement Course (SEC) Theory + Lab", "fm": 60, "filename": "BCA37002_Internet_and_Web_Technologies.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "Which HTML5 semantic element is used to encapsulate self-contained content intended to be independently distributable or reusable (such as a blog post or news article)?", "options": ["<section>", "<article>", "<aside>", "<div>"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "<article> represents an independent, self-contained composition suitable for syndication (e.g. blog post, news story)."}, {"q": "In CSS Box Model, what does the property `box-sizing: border-box` do?", "options": ["Includes padding and border within the specified element width and height", "Adds extra margin equal to border width", "Eliminates the content area completely", "Sets the border style to 3D inset"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "With border-box, the width and height properties include content, padding, and border, preventing layout overflow."}, {"q": "What is the difference between `==` and `===` in JavaScript?", "options": ["`==` compares both value and type; `===` converts types", "`==` performs type coercion before comparison; `===` performs strict comparison without coercion", "`===` is used only for object reference equality", "`==` is deprecated in modern ES6"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Double equal `==` coerces types before checking equality (e.g. '5' == 5 is true); triple equal `===` checks both value and type without coercion."}, {"q": "Which HTTP request method is considered 'idempotent' and 'safe' according to RFC specifications?", "options": ["POST", "GET", "PATCH", "CONNECT"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "GET is safe (does not alter server state) and idempotent (repeated identical requests return identical results without side effects)."}, {"q": "What does the JavaScript event loop do when the Call Stack is empty?", "options": ["Immediately terminates the browser tab", "Pushes the first available callback from the Microtask/Macrotask Callback Queue onto the Call Stack", "Executes synchronous garbage collection", "Freezes DOM updates until next user input"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The Event Loop monitors the call stack; once empty, it dequeues pending microtasks (Promises) then macrotasks (setTimeout, DOM events) to the call stack."}, {"q": "In asynchronous JavaScript, what state does a Promise enter after being resolved successfully?", "options": ["Pending", "Fulfilled", "Rejected", "Settled-to-Null"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A Promise transitions from 'pending' to either 'fulfilled' (resolved with value) or 'rejected' (failed with error)."}, {"q": "Which CSS Flexbox property aligns flex items along the cross axis?", "options": ["justify-content", "align-items", "flex-direction", "flex-wrap"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`justify-content` aligns items along the main axis; `align-items` aligns items along the cross axis."}, {"q": "What is the primary difference between `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` in the Web Storage API?", "options": ["`localStorage` data persists indefinitely until explicitly cleared; `sessionStorage` expires when the browser tab/session closes", "`localStorage` is sent with every HTTP request header; `sessionStorage` is not", "`sessionStorage` can store up to 50MB; `localStorage` is capped at 4KB", "`localStorage` is only accessible through server-side Node.js"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "localStorage data has no expiration date; sessionStorage data is purged immediately when the tab/window session ends."}, {"q": "What does CORS stand for in web security architecture?", "options": ["Centralized Object Resource Standard", "Cross-Origin Resource Sharing", "Client-Oriented Rendering Server", "Cascading Open Routing System"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism using HTTP headers to allow or restrict resources requested across different origins."}, {"q": "Which protocol upgrades an ordinary HTTP connection into a persistent, full-duplex, bidirectional communication channel over a single TCP socket?", "options": ["HTTP/1.1 Keep-Alive", "WebSocket (ws:// / wss://)", "Server-Sent Events (SSE)", "FTP"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "WebSockets initiate an HTTP handshake with Upgrade header and maintain a persistent full-duplex TCP channel."}, {"q": "In responsive web design, what CSS feature allows styling rules to trigger based on device viewport width or screen resolution?", "options": ["CSS Grid Template", "Media Queries (`@media`)", "CSS Pseudo-elements", "Keyframe Animations"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Media queries (`@media screen and (max-width: 768px)`) adapt CSS layout to different screen widths and device characteristics."}, {"q": "What is JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) fundamentally defined as?", "options": ["A binary executable format for web servers", "A lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format", "A client-side relational database", "A CSS preprocessor scripting language"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "JSON is a lightweight, human-readable text format for data exchange derived from JavaScript object syntax but universally supported across languages."}, {"q": "Which method cancels the default browser action of an HTML form submission in JavaScript event handling?", "options": ["event.stopPropagation()", "event.preventDefault()", "event.stopImmediatePropagation()", "return null"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`event.preventDefault()` prevents default browser action (e.g. form reload, following hyperlink) without halting event bubbling."}, {"q": "In CSS Grid Layout, which unit of measurement represents a fraction of the available free space in the grid container?", "options": ["px", "em", "fr", "%"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "The `fr` unit represents a fraction of free leftover space in a CSS grid container (`grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr`)."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of the Document Object Model (DOM) in web browsers?", "options": ["To compile JavaScript into machine bytecode", "To represent the HTML document as an interactive tree structure of objects that can be manipulated by scripts", "To securely encrypt client cookies", "To manage DNS caching for HTTP requests"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "DOM is an API and hierarchical node-tree representation of the HTML document allowing programmatic access and manipulation via JavaScript."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the difference between CSS Flexbox (1D) and CSS Grid (2D) layout models with appropriate use cases.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Flexbox is 1-dimensional (arranging items in a row OR column; navigation bars, item lists). Grid is 2-dimensional (controlling rows AND columns simultaneously; full page layouts, photo galleries)."}, {"q": "What is Closure in JavaScript? Provide a concise code example demonstrating data privacy using closures.", "marks": 3, "hint": "A function bundled with references to its lexical environment. Example: function createCounter() { let count = 0; return () => ++count; }. Outer variable 'count' is encapsulated."}, {"q": "Explain the concept of Asynchronous JavaScript. Contrast Callbacks with Promises and `async/await`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Non-blocking execution. Callbacks suffer from 'callback hell' and inverted control. Promises provide chainable .then() and .catch(). Async/await offers synchronous-looking syntax over Promises."}, {"q": "Describe the HTTP Request-Response lifecycle when a user types `https://example.com` into a web browser.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Steps: 1. DNS resolution (URL -> IP). 2. TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK). 3. TLS handshake. 4. HTTP GET request. 5. Server processing and response. 6. Browser DOM/CSSOM parsing and rendering."}, {"q": "What is AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML)? How has the modern `fetch()` API replaced `XMLHttpRequest`?", "marks": 3, "hint": "AJAX updates parts of a web page asynchronously without reloading the entire page. fetch() provides a Promise-based, cleaner syntax with request/response streaming compared to verbose event-driven XHR."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Client-Side Rendering (CSR) and Server-Side Rendering (SSR).", "marks": 3, "hint": "CSR (React/Vue SPAs): minimal HTML downloaded, browser executes JS to render UI; faster page transitions, poorer initial SEO/FCP. SSR: server renders complete HTML per request; faster first contentful paint and superior SEO."}, {"q": "Explain the Same-Origin Policy (SOP) and how Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) preflight requests operate.", "marks": 3, "hint": "SOP blocks scripts on origin A from accessing resources on origin B (scheme+host+port). CORS relaxes this via headers (Access-Control-Allow-Origin). Preflight OPTIONS request checks allowed methods before non-simple requests."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Design and write the complete HTML5 and CSS3 code for a modern Responsive Navigation Bar. It must display as an inline horizontal menu on desktops (viewport >= 768px) and transform into a mobile hamburger menu on viewports < 768px using CSS Media Queries.", "marks": 5, "hint": "HTML structure with nav, logo, ul.nav-links, and hamburger button. CSS with flexbox, desktop styling, and @media screen and (max-width: 768px) showing how links collapse into vertical block with display toggle."}, {"q": "Explain the JavaScript Event Loop architecture in detail. Describe the Call Stack, Web APIs, Microtask Queue (Promises, queueMicrotask), Macrotask/Task Queue (setTimeout, setInterval, I/O), and explain the exact output of a given complex snippet mixing Promises and timeouts.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Illustrate architecture. Explain priority: synchronous call stack runs to completion -> all microtasks are drained completely -> single macrotask is executed -> render pass -> repeat. Provide trace demonstrating exact execution order."}, {"q": "Write an end-to-end JavaScript module using the modern `fetch()` API and `async/await` to perform CRUD operations against a RESTful API (`https://api.example.com/products`). Include GET, POST with JSON payload, error handling with `try...catch`, and DOM rendering.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write clean async functions: fetchProducts(), createProduct(data). Show headers {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}, JSON.stringify(), response.ok check, error throw, and dynamic element creation/injection into DOM."}, {"q": "Explain DOM Manipulation and Event Propagation in JavaScript. Contrast Event Bubbling with Event Capturing. Write a practical code example illustrating Event Delegation on a dynamic list.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Phases: 1. Capturing (down from window to target). 2. Target phase. 3. Bubbling (up from target to window). Event delegation attaches single listener to parent ul exploiting bubbling: e.target.matches('li') handles dynamically added items in O(1) listener memory."}, {"q": "Analyze Web Application Security vulnerabilities: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS - Stored, Reflected, DOM-based), Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF), and SQL Injection. Detail remediation strategies for each vulnerability.", "marks": 5, "hint": "XSS: injects malicious scripts; fix with context-aware output escaping, CSP headers, HttpOnly cookies. CSRF: unauthorized commands from authenticated user; fix with anti-CSRF SameSite cookies and token validation. SQLi: malicious SQL input; fix with parameterized queries/prepared statements."}, {"q": "Explain Client-Side Storage mechanisms: Cookies, Web Storage (localStorage & sessionStorage), and IndexedDB. Compare their storage capacities, lifespans, accessibility scopes, and performance implications.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Cookies: 4KB, sent with every HTTP header, security flags (Secure, HttpOnly). LocalStorage/SessionStorage: 5-10MB, synchronous key-value strings, no HTTP header overhead. IndexedDB: 50MB-gigabytes, asynchronous transactional NoSQL object store for complex client datasets."}, {"q": "Detail CSS Grid Layout architecture. Explain `grid-template-columns`, `grid-template-rows`, `gap`, `grid-column: span`, `repeat()`, `minmax()`, and `grid-template-areas`. Create a 3-column Holy Grail layout.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define container display: grid. Show holy grail layout: grid-template-areas: 'header header header' 'nav main aside' 'footer footer footer'. Explain auto-fit vs auto-fill with minmax(200px, 1fr) for responsive grid without media queries."}, {"q": "Discuss Progressive Web Apps (PWAs). Explain the role of the Web App Manifest, Service Workers (lifecycle: install, activate, fetch), Cache Storage API for offline operation, and Push Notifications.", "marks": 5, "hint": "PWAs combine web reach with native app capabilities. Manifest defines icons/display mode. Service Worker is a background worker acting as programmable network proxy intercepting fetch requests to serve cached assets offline (Cache-First or Network-First strategies)."}]}, {"id": "MCA37510A", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA37510A", "title": "Android Application Development", "type": "Elective Major Theory + Lab", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA37510A_Android_Application_Development.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "What is the primary execution environment that replaced Dalvik Virtual Machine in modern Android (version 5.0 Lollipop onwards)?", "options": ["Android RunTime (ART) with AOT compilation", "Java HotSpot VM", "Node.js V8 Engine", "GraalVM"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "ART introduced Ahead-Of-Time (AOT) and hybrid JIT compilation, drastically improving app performance and battery efficiency over Dalvik."}, {"q": "Which lifecycle callback method is called immediately before an Activity becomes visible to the user?", "options": ["onCreate()", "onStart()", "onResume()", "onPause()"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "onStart() is invoked as the activity becomes visible; onResume() is called when the user can actively interact with it."}, {"q": "Which Android component handles long-running background operations without providing a user interface?", "options": ["Activity", "Service", "Broadcast Receiver", "Content Provider"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Services run in the background to execute continuous tasks (audio playback, data syncing) without a UI."}, {"q": "In Android Jetpack architecture, what is the role of ViewModel?", "options": ["Directly manipulate SQLite raw cursors", "Store and manage UI-related data in a lifecycle-conscious way so data survives configuration changes like screen rotations", "Render OpenGL graphics on canvas", "Intercept incoming push notification payloads"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "ViewModel survives configuration changes (device rotation), preventing redundant network or database refetching."}, {"q": "Which manifest permission is required to check whether the device has active internet connectivity?", "options": ["ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION", "ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE", "READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE", "BIND_JOB_SERVICE"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE permits checking network status through ConnectivityManager."}, {"q": "What is an Implicit Intent in Android?", "options": ["An intent that specifies the exact target Java/Kotlin class name", "An intent that declares a general action to perform, allowing Android OS to resolve matching apps across the device", "An intent used strictly for in-process memory sharing", "An intent that shuts down the operating system"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Implicit intents specify an action (e.g. ACTION_VIEW, ACTION_SEND) and let the OS launch any installed app matching the intent filter."}, {"q": "In the Room Persistence Library, what does `@Dao` designate?", "options": ["Data Access Object interface defining methods for database CRUD operations", "Database table schema definition", "Data Annotation Output for UI views", "Direct Android Output stream"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "@Dao marks an interface that declares SQL queries and mapping methods (@Insert, @Query, @Delete) for Room."}, {"q": "Which Android layout organizes child elements into a single horizontal or vertical row?", "options": ["ConstraintLayout", "LinearLayout", "FrameLayout", "TableLayout"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "LinearLayout arranges child views in a single linear direction based on android:orientation (horizontal or vertical)."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of `ViewBinding` in modern Android development?", "options": ["To replace slow `findViewById` lookups with compile-time type-safe view references", "To compile Java code into C++", "To execute asynchronous HTTP requests", "To draw vector drawables"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "ViewBinding generates binding classes for each XML layout, offering null-safety and compile-time type safety over findViewById."}, {"q": "Which Android Jetpack library is recommended for deferrable, guaranteed background work even if the app exits or device reboots?", "options": ["AsyncTask", "WorkManager", "IntentService", "Thread.sleep()"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "WorkManager handles deferrable, guaranteed background work respecting battery-saving constraints (Doze mode)."}, {"q": "In Kotlin Coroutines on Android, which Dispatcher is utilized for running CPU-bound operations?", "options": ["Dispatchers.Main", "Dispatchers.IO", "Dispatchers.Default", "Dispatchers.Unconfined"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Dispatchers.Default is backed by a shared pool of threads optimized for CPU-intensive work (sorting lists, parsing JSON)."}, {"q": "What is the file format of compiled Android application packages deployed to devices?", "options": [".jar", ".apk / .aab", ".exe", ".war"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Android applications are packaged as APK (Android Package) or Android App Bundle (AAB)."}, {"q": "Which component allows data sharing between different applications securely (e.g. accessing device Contacts)?", "options": ["BroadcastReceiver", "ContentProvider", "PendingIntent", "JobScheduler"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "ContentProviders encapsulate data and provide standard REST-like URIs (content://) for inter-app data access."}, {"q": "What is RecyclerView's primary efficiency advantage over traditional ListView?", "options": ["It natively renders 3D animations", "It mandates the ViewHolder pattern and recycles off-screen views to minimize expensive view inflation", "It does not require an adapter", "It only supports static arrays"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "RecyclerView recycles view holders as items scroll off-screen, avoiding repeated inflation and memory spikes."}, {"q": "Which HTTP client library is widely used in Android to turn REST APIs into type-safe Kotlin/Java interfaces?", "options": ["Retrofit", "Glide", "Picasso", "Room"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Square's Retrofit converts REST APIs into type-safe interfaces using annotations (@GET, @POST) and OkHttp under the hood."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Draw and explain the Android Activity Lifecycle states and callbacks.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Flow: onCreate() -> onStart() -> onResume() -> (Active/Running) -> onPause() -> onStop() -> onDestroy(). Include onRestart() upon returning from background."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Explicit Intents and Implicit Intents with code examples.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Explicit: specifies target component Intent(context, TargetActivity.class). Implicit: specifies action Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse('https://...')) allowing OS resolution."}, {"q": "Explain the architectural components of Android Jetpack MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Model (Repository, Room, Retrofit), ViewModel (exposes LiveData/StateFlow, survives config changes), View (Activity/Fragment observing state and reacting)."}, {"q": "What is a Broadcast Receiver in Android? Contrast Normal Broadcasts with Ordered Broadcasts.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Listens for system/app broadcast events (e.g. BOOT_COMPLETED, BATTERY_LOW). Normal: asynchronous, parallel. Ordered: propagated sequentially by priority, can abort."}, {"q": "Describe the Room Persistence Library architecture: `@Entity`, `@Dao`, and `@Database`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "@Entity defines SQLite table schema; @Dao provides CRUD query methods; @Database acts as main access point connecting entities and DAOs."}, {"q": "What are Fragments? Explain how Fragment Lifecycle corresponds to its parent Activity Lifecycle.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Modular, reusable sub-activity UI portion. Has its own view lifecycle (onCreateView, onViewCreated, onDestroyView) nested within activity lifecycle."}, {"q": "Explain the role of Kotlin Coroutines in modern Android development. Why are they called 'lightweight threads'?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Coroutines execute non-blocking asynchronous code sequentially without callback hell. Thousands of coroutines can share few OS threads with minimal memory overhead."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Design and implement a complete Android News Feed screen using `RecyclerView`. Write the code for the XML item layout, the `RecyclerView.Adapter`, and the `ViewHolder` pattern managing image loading and click events.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Detail layout news_item.xml. Write NewsAdapter extending RecyclerView.Adapter<NewsViewHolder>. Implement onCreateViewHolder (inflates layout), onBindViewHolder (binds title, description, uses Glide/Picasso for image), and getItemCount()."}, {"q": "Explain Android Room Database implementation with asynchronous Coroutines/Flow. Write code for an entity `User`, a DAO with `@Insert` and `@Query(\"SELECT * FROM user_table\")`, and the abstract `AppDatabase` class.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write Kotlin code: @Entity(tableName='user_table') data class User(@PrimaryKey(autoGenerate=true) val id: Int, val name: String, val email: String). DAO interface with suspend fun insert(user: User) and fun getAllUsers(): Flow<List<User>>. Database class with singleton instance."}, {"q": "Explain how Retrofit 2 interacts with RESTful web services in Android. Demonstrate interface definitions with `@GET` and `@POST`, GsonConverterFactory configuration, OkHttpClient logging interceptors, and handling responses with Kotlin Coroutines.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write Retrofit client builder with baseUrl and addConverterFactory(GsonConverterFactory.create()). Define interface ApiService: @GET('/posts') suspend fun getPosts(): List<Post>. Detail error handling with try-catch or Result wrapper."}, {"q": "Discuss Android Security Best Practices: Scoped Storage (Storage Access Framework), Runtime Permissions model (dangerous vs normal permissions), ProGuard/R8 code obfuscation, and secure data storage using EncryptedSharedPreferences.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Scoped storage isolates app private storage. Runtime permissions request user approval at runtime using ActivityResultContracts. R8 shrinks, optimizes, and obfuscates bytecode. EncryptedSharedPreferences uses Android Keystore for AES-256 GCM encryption."}, {"q": "Explain the Service component in Android. Differentiate between Foreground Service, Background Service, and Bound Service. Explain how Foreground Services require user notifications to ensure transparency.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Foreground service performs user-noticeable operations (GPS tracking, media playback) and MUST display an ongoing notification with startForeground(). Background services are throttled by OS in Android 8+. Bound services provide client-server IPC interface."}, {"q": "Compare Jetpack Compose (Declarative UI) with traditional XML-based Imperative UI. Detail `@Composable` functions, state management using `remember` and `mutableStateOf()`, and Recomposition mechanics.", "marks": 5, "hint": "XML is imperative (mutate views via setters); Compose is declarative (UI = f(state)). Compose functions re-execute (recompose) when observed state changes. Write sample composable with Column, Text, and Button modifying counter state."}, {"q": "Explain Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) integration in Android applications. Detail token generation, upstream vs downstream messaging, payload types (Notification vs Data payload), and background message handling via `FirebaseMessagingService`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Explain device registration token onNewToken(). Notification payload auto-displayed by system tray when app in background; Data payload delivered to onMessageReceived() allowing custom background handling. Create NotificationChannel for Android 8+."}, {"q": "Analyze WorkManager for guaranteed deferrable background processing. Detail `Worker` / `CoroutineWorker`, `WorkRequest` (OneTimeWorkRequest, PeriodicWorkRequest), and `Constraints` (NetworkType.CONNECTED, RequiresCharging).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Explain doWork() method returning Result.success() or Result.retry(). Set constraints using Constraints.Builder().setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.CONNECTED). Enqueue via WorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(request)."}]}, {"id": "MCA30511A", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA30511A", "title": "Blockchain Technology", "type": "Elective Major Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA30511A_Blockchain_Technology.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "Which cryptographic hash function is utilized in Bitcoin for block header hashing and address generation?", "options": ["MD5", "SHA-1", "Double SHA-256", "AES-256"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Bitcoin uses SHA-256(SHA-256(data)) for block hashing and Proof-of-Work computations."}, {"q": "What data structure is utilized in a block header to summarize and verify the integrity of all transactions in that block efficiently?", "options": ["B-Tree", "Merkle Tree (Binary Hash Tree)", "Red-Black Tree", "Linked List"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A Merkle Tree computes hierarchical cryptographic hashes of transactions up to a single 32-byte Merkle Root stored in the block header."}, {"q": "In Proof of Work (PoW) consensus, what is the 'Nonce'?", "options": ["The cryptographic public key of the miner", "A 32-bit arbitrary number varied by miners to obtain a block hash below the difficulty target", "The private seed phrase of the wallet", "The block timestamp in milliseconds"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Nonce ('number used once') is an arbitrary counter incremented until the double SHA-256 hash yields the required leading zeros."}, {"q": "What is the primary vulnerability of Proof of Work blockchains where an entity controls over half of the network's hash power?", "options": ["Sybil Attack", "51% Attack (Double Spending)", "Eclipse Attack", "Replay Attack"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Controlling >50% hash rate enables rewriting recent blockchain history, facilitating double-spending and transaction reorganization."}, {"q": "Which consensus mechanism elects block validators proportionally to their economic stake committed in native cryptocurrency?", "options": ["Proof of Work (PoW)", "Proof of Stake (PoS)", "Proof of Authority (PoA)", "Delegated Byzantine Fault Tolerance (dBFT)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "In PoS (e.g. Ethereum 2.0), validators lock up collateral tokens as stake to participate in block proposing and attestation."}, {"q": "What is Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM)?", "options": ["A physical data center hosting blockchain nodes", "A quasi-Turing complete runtime environment that executes smart contract bytecode across Ethereum nodes", "An operating system for mining ASICs", "A compiler for translating C++ to Rust"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "EVM is a decentralized stack-based virtual machine executing smart contract bytecode deterministically across all network nodes."}, {"q": "In Ethereum smart contract execution, what is 'Gas'?", "options": ["A fee unit measuring the computational effort required to execute a transaction or opcode", "A physical fuel for mining rigs", "The exchange rate of Ether against USD", "A hard fork protocol update"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Gas measures execution steps and prevents infinite loops (Halting problem) by halting transactions when allocated gas is exhausted."}, {"q": "Which Solidity keyword designates a function that cannot modify or read the blockchain state?", "options": ["view", "pure", "payable", "external"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`pure` functions do not read or modify state variables; `view` functions read state but cannot modify it."}, {"q": "What is the standard token interface for fungible tokens on the Ethereum network?", "options": ["ERC-20", "ERC-721", "ERC-1155", "BIP-39"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "ERC-20 defines the standard API for fungible tokens (totalSupply, balanceOf, transfer, approve, transferFrom)."}, {"q": "Which Ethereum standard defines non-fungible tokens (NFTs) representing unique digital assets?", "options": ["ERC-20", "ERC-721", "ERC-223", "ERC-777"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "ERC-721 specifies unique token identifiers (tokenId) representing non-fungible, distinct digital collectibles."}, {"q": "In Hyperledger Fabric, which identity and security component manages PKI certificates for network participants?", "options": ["Fabric CA (Certificate Authority)", "Orderer Node", "Chaincode Engine", "Gossip Protocol"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Fabric CA issues digital X.509 identity certificates establishing cryptographic identity and Role-Based Access Control in permissioned consortiums."}, {"q": "What problem does the Byzantine Generals Problem illustrate in distributed computing?", "options": ["How to encrypt data at rest", "How distributed nodes can reliably reach consensus over an unreliable network in the presence of malicious/traitorous actors", "How to compress blocks in a chain", "How to allocate CPU cores for multi-threading"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Byzantine fault tolerance addresses consensus across distributed systems where participants may crash or send conflicting false information."}, {"q": "What is the UTXO model utilized by Bitcoin?", "options": ["Universal Token Exchange Object", "Unspent Transaction Output model", "Unified Transfer Protocol", "User Temporary XML Output"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Bitcoin tracks transactions as a directed acyclic graph of Unspent Transaction Outputs (UTXOs) rather than account balances."}, {"q": "Which famous vulnerability caused the 2016 Ethereum DAO attack, leading to the hard fork between Ethereum and Ethereum Classic?", "options": ["Integer Overflow", "Reentrancy Vulnerability", "Front-running", "Private Key Collision"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Reentrancy allows an external fallback function to recursively call the withdraw function before the user's internal balance is decremented."}, {"q": "Which zero-knowledge cryptographic protocol allows a prover to convince a verifier that a statement is true without revealing any underlying secret data?", "options": ["zk-SNARKs", "Diffie-Hellman", "RSA-4096", "SHA-3"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "zk-SNARKs (Zero-Knowledge Succinct Non-Interactive Argument of Knowledge) provide mathematical proofs of statement validity without leaking private data."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the structure of a Block in a blockchain (Block Header components and Body).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Header: Previous Block Hash, Merkle Root Hash, Timestamp, Difficulty Target, Nonce. Body: List of verified transactions."}, {"q": "Describe how Merkle Trees verify transaction inclusion in O(log n) time without downloading the entire blockchain.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Simplified Payment Verification (SPV). Client only needs the Merkle Root, target tx hash, and log2(N) sibling hashes along the Merkle audit path to verify integrity."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Public (Permissionless), Private (Permissioned), and Consortium Blockchains.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Public (Bitcoin, Ethereum): anonymous, anyone can read/write, slow throughput. Private (Hyperledger Fabric): known identities, access controlled by single entity, high speed. Consortium: multi-organization governing nodes."}, {"q": "Explain Proof of Work (PoW) vs Proof of Stake (PoS). Why is PoS substantially more energy-efficient?", "marks": 3, "hint": "PoW relies on brute-force electrical hash computation by ASICs. PoS selects validators based on staked capital; block proposing requires minimal compute energy."}, {"q": "What is a Smart Contract? How does it enforce trustless transaction execution?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Self-executing digital code deployed on blockchain. Executes deterministically when pre-programmed conditions are met without intermediaries or third-party escrow."}, {"q": "Explain the concept of Gas, Gas Limit, and Gas Price in the Ethereum network.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Gas: computational work units. Gas Limit: max units user is willing to spend. Gas Price: fee paid per unit (in Gwei). Total fee = Gas Used * Gas Price. Prevents infinite loops."}, {"q": "Describe the UTXO model vs Account/Balance model used by Bitcoin and Ethereum respectively.", "marks": 3, "hint": "UTXO: transactions consume previous unspent outputs and create new ones (immutable state, high privacy). Account model: stateful bank account model with persistent balances (simpler smart contracts, less storage overhead)."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Explain the mechanics of a Reentrancy Attack on Ethereum smart contracts. Write a vulnerable Solidity contract snippet, explain how the attack exploits the fallback/receive function, and provide the secure remediation using the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern or ReentrancyGuard.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Vulnerable code transfers ETH via call.value() before updating balances[msg.sender]=0. Remediation: update state variables BEFORE making external calls (Checks-Effects-Interactions) or apply OpenZeppelin's nonReentrant modifier."}, {"q": "Detail the architecture of Hyperledger Fabric. Explain the roles of Peer Nodes (Endorsing Peers, Committing Peers), Orderer Nodes (Raft consensus), Channels, Fabric CA, and Chaincode execution flow.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Execute-Order-Validate architecture. Client sends proposal -> Endorsing peers simulate chaincode and return signed endorsements -> Orderer sequences endorsed txs into blocks -> Committing peers validate endorsement policy and commit block to ledger."}, {"q": "Write a complete, compilable ERC-20 compliant Token Smart Contract in Solidity. Implement `balanceOf`, `transfer`, `approve`, and `transferFrom` functions along with Transfer and Approval events.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write Solidity 0.8+ contract with mapping(address => uint256) balances and mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) allowed. Implement safe transfer logic, checking balance, debiting sender, crediting recipient, and emitting Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount)."}, {"q": "Analyze Blockchain Scalability challenges. Compare Layer-1 scaling solutions (Sharding, Proof-of-Stake migration) with Layer-2 scaling solutions (Optimistic Rollups, ZK-Rollups, State Channels).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Blockchain Trilemma (Decentralization, Security, Scalability). L1: Sharding splits state/transactions across parallel chains. L2: Rollups execute transactions off-chain and post compressed state proofs (fraud proofs for Optimistic, validity proofs/zk-SNARKs for ZK-Rollups) to L1."}, {"q": "Discuss Cryptographic primitives in Blockchain: Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECDSA secp256k1), Public-Private key pair generation, and the mathematical procedure for deriving a Bitcoin wallet address from a private key.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Private key k (256-bit random). Public key K = k * G (point multiplication on secp256k1 curve). Bitcoin address: K -> SHA-256 -> RIPEMD-160 -> prepend 0x00 version byte -> compute checksum via double SHA-256 -> Base58Check encoding."}, {"q": "Explain Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) consensus algorithm. Detail the three phases (Pre-prepare, Prepare, Commit), quorum thresholds, and why it can tolerate up to f faulty nodes in a system with 3f + 1 total nodes.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Phase 1: Pre-prepare (leader broadcasts proposal). Phase 2: Prepare (nodes broadcast prepare messages, need 2f+1 to agree). Phase 3: Commit (nodes broadcast commit, 2f+1 commit messages ensure global ordering). Tolerate f malicious nodes because 3f+1 - f = 2f+1 guarantees quorum overlap."}, {"q": "Discuss Decentralized Finance (DeFi) primitives: Automated Market Makers (AMM) using the constant product formula `x * y = k` (Uniswap), Liquidity Pools, Impermanent Loss, and Flash Loans.", "marks": 5, "hint": "AMM replaces order books with algorithmic pools. Constant product: (R_x + delta_x)(R_y - delta_y) = k determines swap price. Impermanent loss occurs when pool asset price diverges from external market. Flash loans allow uncollateralized borrowing within a single atomic transaction."}, {"q": "Analyze Blockchain applications in Supply Chain Management and Healthcare. Detail how immutability, provenance tracking, cryptographic attestations, and zero-knowledge identity solve counterfeit pharmaceuticals and medical records sharing.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Pharma: track serial numbers and temperature sensors at each custodian handover via smart contracts, preventing counterfeit entry. Healthcare: patient owns private key controlling ACL permissions over encrypted electronic health records (EHR) stored on IPFS."}]}, {"id": "AEC00006", "programme": "BCA & BCA (AI & ML) - Semester III - 2026", "code": "AEC00006", "title": "Modern Indian Language (Alternative English)", "type": "Ability Enhancement Course (AEC) Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "AEC00006_Alternative_English.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "In business communication, what is the '7 Cs' principle that advises providing all necessary facts so the recipient can take appropriate action?", "options": ["Conciseness", "Completeness", "Courtesy", "Concreteness"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Completeness ensures that the message conveys all necessary facts, figures, and context needed for the receiver to react properly."}, {"q": "Which reading technique involves rapidly surveying a text to locate a specific piece of information (such as a date or phone number)?", "options": ["Skimming", "Scanning", "Intensive Reading", "Critical Reading"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Scanning is searching specifically for a particular keyword, date, or fact, whereas Skimming grasps the general gist."}, {"q": "What is an active voice sentence among the following?", "options": ["The server was configured by the system administrator.", "The system administrator configured the server.", "A software patch was deployed overnight.", "The error logs are being inspected."], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "In active voice, the grammatical subject performs the action directly: 'The administrator configured the server'."}, {"q": "What is the primary objective of a 'Pr\u00e9cis' in formal writing?", "options": ["To critique and challenge the author's viewpoint", "To write a concise, faithful summary of a passage in approximately one-third of the original word count", "To expand a short quotation into a full essay", "To translate archaic prose into modern slang"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A pr\u00e9cis is a clear, concise, objective summary preserving the original author's core ideas in roughly one-third length."}, {"q": "Which part of a formal business email summarizes the main purpose in a few clear words before opening?", "options": ["Salutation", "Subject Line", "Sign-off", "Enclosure"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The subject line provides a concise, specific preview of the message content and urgency."}, {"q": "Choose the correctly spelled technical word:", "options": ["Accomodation", "Accommodation", "Acommodation", "Accommadation"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The correct spelling is 'Accommodation' (double c, double m)."}, {"q": "Identify the figure of speech in: 'The firewall stood as an unyielding fortress guarding the corporate network.'", "options": ["Simile", "Metaphor", "Personification", "Hyperbole"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A metaphor asserts that one thing is another directly ('firewall stood as a fortress' without using 'like' or 'as')."}, {"q": "What type of report provides factual information and recommendations following an investigation of an incident or feasibility study?", "options": ["Analytical/Investigative Report", "Narrative Memoir", "Literary Monograph", "Fictional Narrative"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Analytical and investigative business reports examine data, identify root causes, and recommend actionable solutions."}, {"q": "Which idiom means 'to resolve a conflict and restore harmonious relations'?", "options": ["Bite the bullet", "Bury the hatchet", "Burn the midnight oil", "Bark up the wrong tree"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "'To bury the hatchet' means to settle past differences and establish peace."}, {"q": "In a formal curriculum vitae (CV) or resume, which section highlights demonstrable job accomplishments and technical proficiencies?", "options": ["Hobbies and Interests", "Professional Experience & Skills", "Personal Declaration", "Signature and Date"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Professional experience and technical skills provide concrete evidence of competence and achievements to recruiters."}, {"q": "Identify the grammatically correct sentence:", "options": ["Neither of the two candidates have completed their interview.", "Neither of the two candidates has completed his or her interview.", "Neither candidate are qualified.", "Neither of them were present yesterday."], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "'Neither' as a distributive pronoun takes a singular verb: 'has completed'."}, {"q": "What is the tone required in official memorandums and formal corporate correspondence?", "options": ["Casual, humorous, and colloquial", "Objective, professional, clear, and polite", "Confrontational and emotional", "Poetic and figurative"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Corporate correspondence demands objective clarity, conciseness, and polite professional decorum."}, {"q": "What is an antonym for the word 'METICULOUS'?", "options": ["Scrupulous", "Careless", "Precise", "Thorough"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Meticulous means showing great attention to detail; its opposite is careless or negligent."}, {"q": "Which punctuation mark is correctly used to introduce a formal list or explanation following an independent clause?", "options": ["Semicolon (;)", "Colon (:)", "Comma (,)", "Hyphen (-)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "A colon introduces a formal list, quotation, or explanatory elaboration following a complete independent clause."}, {"q": "What does non-verbal communication include?", "options": ["Spoken phone conversations", "Written emails and documents", "Body language, facial expressions, eye contact, and posture", "Syntax and grammatical vocabulary"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Non-verbal communication encompasses kinesics (body language), proxemics, posture, and facial cues."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain the 7 Cs of Effective Business Communication with brief descriptions of each.", "marks": 3, "hint": "1. Completeness, 2. Conciseness, 3. Consideration, 4. Clarity, 5. Concreteness, 6. Courtesy, 7. Correctness."}, {"q": "Differentiate between Skimming and Scanning reading strategies with practical scenarios.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Skimming: rapid reading to grasp general main ideas (e.g. browsing news headlines). Scanning: targeted visual search for specific data points (e.g. flight arrival time on board)."}, {"q": "Write a formal email requesting leave of absence for 3 days due to an urgent family medical emergency.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Include clear subject line, polite formal salutation, explicit dates of absence, delegation/handover of urgent duties, contact availability, professional sign-off."}, {"q": "Explain the barriers to effective communication (Physical, Psychological, Linguistic/Semantic, Cultural).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Physical (noise, distance), Psychological (prejudices, stress), Semantic (jargon, ambiguous phrasing), Cultural (differing body language conventions)."}, {"q": "Rewrite the following sentences into clear Active Voice:<br/>1. The system architecture was approved by the technical lead.<br/>2. An unexpected vulnerability was discovered by the security auditor.<br/>3. Detailed instructions will be provided by our HR manager.", "marks": 3, "hint": "1. The technical lead approved the system architecture. 2. The security auditor discovered an unexpected vulnerability. 3. Our HR manager will provide detailed instructions."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a Resume and a Curriculum Vitae (CV)?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Resume: concise 1-2 page summary of skills and relevant work experience tailored to specific job. CV: comprehensive, detailed historical record of entire academic and professional credentials."}, {"q": "Explain the importance of Non-Verbal Communication (Body Language, Eye Contact, Posture) in technical job interviews.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Non-verbal cues project confidence, sincerity, and active listening. Good posture and steady eye contact convey composure under pressure."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Write a comprehensive formal Business Report addressed to the Chief Information Officer (CIO) on the Feasibility of Migrating On-Premise Enterprise Servers to Cloud Infrastructure (AWS/Azure). Include: Title, Executive Summary, Current Infrastructure Limitations, Cost-Benefit Analysis, Risk Assessment, and Strategic Recommendations.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Structure with formal headings: 1. Title Page & To/From headers. 2. Executive Summary. 3. Current Pain Points (aging hardware, downtime). 4. Financial Analysis (CapEx vs OpEx). 5. Security & SLA Risks. 6. Actionable phased migration roadmap."}, {"q": "Write a Pr\u00e9cis of the following passage in approximately one-third of its length. Supply an appropriate title:<br/><i>'Technological revolutions have continually reshaped human employment and productivity throughout history. The current wave of artificial intelligence and automation has provoked anxieties regarding the obsolescence of human labor. However, historical precedents demonstrate that while technology disrupts repetitive tasks, it simultaneously creates new industries, demands advanced cognitive capabilities, and improves standard of living. Rather than replacing human intellect, AI serves as an augmentative amplifier. The workforce must adapt through lifelong learning and upskilling, mastering human-centric domains such as ethical judgment, creative problem-solving, and emotional empathy that algorithms cannot replicate.'</i>", "marks": 5, "hint": "Suggested Title: 'AI and the Future of Human Labor'. Pr\u00e9cis (approx 45 words): While AI-driven automation raises fears of job loss, historical precedents show technology enhances human productivity rather than eradicating work. By augmenting intelligence, AI necessitates workforce upskilling in irreplaceable human strengths like creative problem solving, ethics, and empathy."}, {"q": "Draft a professional Cover Letter and structured Resume applying for the position of 'Junior Full Stack Developer / Software Engineer' at a leading IT firm. Highlight technical proficiencies, project experience, and academic qualifications.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Format Cover Letter with applicant and employer addresses, reference to job post, match between candidate skills and company goals. Structure Resume: Objective, Education, Technical Skills (Python, React, SQL), Academic Projects, Certifications."}, {"q": "Write a persuasive essay (approx 250 words) on: 'Ethics and Governance in Generative Artificial Intelligence: Striking the Balance Between Innovation and Responsibility'.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Essay structure: Introduction (rise of GenAI like LLMs, deepfakes), Body Paragraph 1 (benefits: productivity, coding, medicine), Body Paragraph 2 (ethical perils: bias, copyright infringement, misinformation), Body Paragraph 3 (regulatory frameworks: EU AI Act, watermarking), Conclusion (responsible stewardship)."}, {"q": "Draft a formal Memorandum (Memo) from the Head of the IT Department to all university staff outlining a mandatory Cyber Security Policy against Phishing Attacks, Password Hygiene, and Multi-Factor Authentication protocols.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Standard Memo layout: TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT. Bullet points outlining: 1. Identification of suspicious phishing links, 2. Mandatory 14-character alphanumeric passwords changed quarterly, 3. Compulsory 2FA setup on institutional email, 4. Protocol for reporting security breaches."}, {"q": "Explain the structure and dynamics of Group Discussions (GD) conducted during corporate recruitment. Discuss key evaluation parameters (Subject Knowledge, Leadership, Communication Skills, Conflict Resolution) and strategies for effective participation.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Detail GD setup. Parameters: clarity of expression, active listening, body language, domain knowledge. Strategies: effective initiation, synthesizing diverse viewpoints, politely intervening to steer off-topic debates, concluding with a coherent group summary."}, {"q": "Read the following scenario and formulate a professional Crisis Communication Strategy:<br/>An e-commerce company suffers an unauthorized data breach exposing customer phone numbers and hashed passwords. Draft an official Press Release addressing customer concerns, outlining containment actions taken, and offering free identity protection services.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Draft Press Release: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE header, Dateline, transparent acknowledgment of incident, reassurance that payment info was not breached, immediate remediation (patching, law enforcement engaged), customer protective steps, dedicated support helpline."}, {"q": "Analyze the role of Interpersonal Communication and Emotional Intelligence (EQ) in software engineering team environments. How do self-awareness, active listening, and constructive code review feedback foster productive collaborative agile teams?", "marks": 5, "hint": "Discuss Daniel Goleman's EQ domains (self-awareness, empathy, social skills). Relate to agile scrums: avoiding toxic code review comments ('this code is terrible' vs 'consider this optimization for O(n) scaling'), fostering psychological safety, collaborative problem solving."}]}, {"id": "BIL37204", "programme": "BCA in Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning - 2026", "code": "BIL37204", "title": "Python Programming", "type": "Core Minor Integrated Theory + Lab", "fm": 60, "filename": "BIL37204_Python_Programming.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "Which of the following built-in Python data types is strictly IMMUTABLE?", "options": ["List", "Dictionary", "Tuple", "Set"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "Tuples, strings, integers, and frozensets are immutable; lists, dictionaries, and sets can be mutated in place."}, {"q": "What is the output of the Python expression: `print([i**2 for i in range(5) if i % 2 != 0])`?", "options": ["[0, 1, 4, 9, 16]", "[1, 9]", "[1, 4, 9]", "[0, 4, 16]"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Range(5) yields 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. Odd numbers are 1 and 3. Their squares are 1**2 = 1 and 3**2 = 9. Output: [1, 9]."}, {"q": "In Python, which dunder (magic) method is called to initialize a newly instantiated class object?", "options": ["__new__()", "__init__()", "__str__()", "__call__()"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "__init__() acts as the constructor initializing attributes of an already created object instance."}, {"q": "What does the `*args` parameter in a Python function definition permit?", "options": ["Passing a variable number of positional arguments as a tuple", "Passing keyword arguments as a dictionary", "Forcing arguments to be floating-point numbers", "Compiling the function to C code"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "*args packs arbitrary positional arguments into a tuple; **kwargs packs keyword arguments into a dictionary."}, {"q": "Which keyword is used in Python to define a generator function that yields a stream of values lazily?", "options": ["return", "yield", "generate", "lazy"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`yield` suspends the function's execution state and yields an iterator value, resuming when `next()` is called."}, {"q": "What is the time complexity of looking up a key in a Python dictionary with a good hash distribution?", "options": ["O(1) average case", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n log n)"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Python dictionaries are hash tables providing average O(1) time complexity for key insertion and lookup."}, {"q": "Which NumPy method reshapes a 1D array of 12 elements into a 2D matrix of 3 rows and 4 columns without copying data?", "options": ["arr.flatten(3, 4)", "arr.reshape(3, 4)", "arr.resize(3, 4)", "arr.split(3, 4)"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`arr.reshape(3, 4)` returns a new view of the array with specified dimensions having the same underlying memory buffer."}, {"q": "What is a Python 'Decorator' fundamentally defined as?", "options": ["A graphical widget in Tkinter", "A higher-order function that takes another function as an argument, extends its behavior, and returns a modified function", "A built-in JSON parser", "A syntax error handler"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Decorators wrap existing functions to dynamically modify or augment their behavior without altering their source code."}, {"q": "In Python, what is the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)?", "options": ["A cryptographic cipher for source files", "A mutex mechanism that restricts execution of CPython bytecode to only one native thread at a time", "A lock on database tables", "A security firewall for socket programming"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "CPython's GIL ensures that only one native OS thread executes Python bytecode at any given moment to protect memory management."}, {"q": "What is the result of slicing `s = 'BRAINWARE'` with `s[::-1]`?", "options": ["'BRAINWARE'", "'ERA WNIARB'", "'ERA WNIAR'", "'ERAWNIARB'"], "ans": "(D)", "expl": "Step -1 reverses the string: 'BRAINWARE' reversed is 'ERAWNIARB'."}, {"q": "Which clause in a `try...except...else...finally` block executes strictly when NO exception is raised?", "options": ["try", "except", "else", "finally"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "The `else` block executes only if the `try` block completes successfully without raising any exceptions."}, {"q": "Which Python module provides support for object serialization and deserialization into byte streams?", "options": ["os", "pickle", "math", "sys"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`pickle` converts Python object hierarchies into byte streams and vice versa."}, {"q": "What does NumPy's array broadcasting allow?", "options": ["Broadcasting audio over WiFi", "Arithmetic operations on arrays of differing shapes subject to compatible dimension constraints", "Converting arrays into Pandas dataframes", "Executing code across cloud clusters"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Broadcasting describes how NumPy treats arrays with different shapes during arithmetic operations without unnecessary data copying."}, {"q": "Which Pandas function is used to pivot and aggregate tabular data into a spreadsheet-style pivot table?", "options": ["pd.melt()", "pd.pivot_table()", "pd.concat()", "pd.crosstab()"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`pivot_table()` aggregates data across index rows and columns using specified aggregation functions."}, {"q": "In OOP, what does the `@staticmethod` decorator signify?", "options": ["The method receives the instance `self` automatically", "The method receives the class `cls` automatically", "The method does not receive an implicit first argument (`self` or `cls`) and behaves like a plain function inside class namespace", "The method can never be called"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "@staticmethod belongs to the class namespace but does not access or modify instance state (`self`) or class state (`cls`)."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Explain List Comprehensions and Dictionary Comprehensions in Python with concise examples.", "marks": 3, "hint": "List: [x**2 for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0]. Dict: {k: v**2 for k, v in [('a', 1), ('b', 2)]}. Concise, faster than explicit loops."}, {"q": "Differentiate between `deepcopy()` and `copy()` (shallow copy) in Python with a nested list example.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Shallow copy copies the outer container, but nested mutable objects share references. Deepcopy recursively duplicates all child objects, creating totally detached copies."}, {"q": "What is a Python Generator? Write a generator function that yields the Fibonacci sequence up to N terms.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Uses `yield` to return values lazily without allocating entire sequence in memory. Code: def fib(n): a, b = 0, 1; for _ in range(n): yield a; a, b = b, a + b."}, {"q": "Explain the Method Resolution Order (MRO) in Python multiple inheritance and the C3 Linearization algorithm.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Defines the search order of classes when resolving methods. Accessible via `ClassName.mro()`. C3 linearization guarantees monotonicity and preserves local precedence order."}, {"q": "What are Python Lambda functions? When are they used with `map()` and `filter()`?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Anonymous, single-expression functions: lambda x: x * 2. Example: list(map(lambda x: x*2, [1, 2, 3])) and list(filter(lambda x: x > 5, nums))."}, {"q": "Explain NumPy Array Broadcasting rules with an example of adding a (3, 1) vector to a (1, 4) vector.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Two dimensions are compatible when: 1. They are equal, or 2. One of them is 1. Shape (3, 1) and (1, 4) broadcast to an output matrix of shape (3, 4)."}, {"q": "Describe the Python `with` statement and Context Managers (`__enter__` and `__exit__`).", "marks": 3, "hint": "Ensures deterministic resource allocation and cleanup (e.g. file closing, mutex unlocking). __enter__() initializes resource; __exit__() guarantees cleanup even if exceptions occur."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Write a complete Python script implementing a custom Decorator `@execution_timer` that calculates and logs the execution duration of any function. Apply it to an algorithm computing prime numbers up to 100,000.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write functools.wraps decorator: def execution_timer(func): @wraps(func) def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): start = time.perf_counter(); result = func(*args, **kwargs); print(f'Elapsed: {time.perf_counter() - start:.6f}s'); return result; return wrapper."}, {"q": "Explain Object-Oriented Programming principles in Python: Encapsulation (private `__` and protected `_` attributes), Polymorphism, and Inheritance. Write a Python class hierarchy demonstrating an abstract base class `Shape` with subclasses `Circle` and `Rectangle` overriding `area()` and `perimeter()`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Use `from abc import ABC, abstractmethod`. Define class Shape(ABC) with @abstractmethod def area(self). Implement Circle and Rectangle with math.pi * r**2 and w * h. Demonstrate polymorphic call across a list of shapes."}, {"q": "Demonstrate data analysis with Pandas and NumPy. Write code to:<br/>1. Load a CSV dataset into a DataFrame.<br/>2. Identify and impute missing values with column medians.<br/>3. Filter rows where 'Age' > 25 and 'Salary' > 50,000.<br/>4. Group by 'Department' and compute mean salary and total headcount.<br/>5. Export results to an Excel/Parquet file.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Write code: df = pd.read_csv('data.csv'); df['Age'].fillna(df['Age'].median(), inplace=True); filtered = df[(df['Age'] > 25) & (df['Salary'] > 50000)]; grouped = filtered.groupby('Department').agg(mean_salary=('Salary', 'mean'), headcount=('Salary', 'count')); grouped.to_parquet('summary.parquet')."}, {"q": "Explain Exception Handling mechanics in Python. Detail custom exception creation by inheriting from `Exception`. Write a banking application scenario where an `InsufficientFundsError` is raised and caught gracefully.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Define class InsufficientFundsError(Exception): pass. In BankAccount.withdraw(amount): if amount > self.balance: raise InsufficientFundsError('Overdraft limit exceeded'). Wrap in try...except InsufficientFundsError as e: print(e)."}, {"q": "Explain Multithreading vs Multiprocessing in Python. Detail why CPU-bound tasks do not benefit from Python's `threading` module due to the GIL, and write code using `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor` to parallelize a compute-heavy task across all CPU cores.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Threading shares memory space but is serialized by CPython GIL; optimal for I/O-bound tasks. Multiprocessing spawns separate OS processes with independent GILs. Write ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=os.cpu_count()) mapping compute function across chunks."}, {"q": "Demonstrate File I/O and JSON manipulation in Python. Write code to read a large JSON log file line-by-line, extract failed authentication attempts (status == 'FAILED'), count occurrences by IP address using `collections.defaultdict`, and write an alert report to a text file.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Use streaming with open('log.jsonl', 'r') as f: for line in f: record = json.loads(line); if record.get('status') == 'FAILED': ip_counts[record['ip']] += 1. Write sorted summary report to alert.txt."}, {"q": "Explain Metaclasses in Python. How does `type` act as the metaclass of all classes? Write a simple metaclass that enforces all attribute names in a class to be lowercase, raising an error upon class declaration otherwise.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Classes are instances of metaclasses (default is `type`). Metaclass __new__(cls, name, bases, dct) inspects dct.keys(). If any key contains uppercase characters (ignoring dunders), raise AttributeError."}, {"q": "Discuss Python Data Visualization using Matplotlib and Seaborn. Write code to generate a 2x2 subplot figure containing:<br/>1. A histogram with KDE curve of a normal distribution.<br/>2. A scatter plot with a linear regression trendline.<br/>3. A categorical boxplot.<br/>4. A heatmap of a correlation matrix.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Code: fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(10, 8)); sns.histplot(data, kde=True, ax=axes[0,0]); sns.regplot(x='x', y='y', data=df, ax=axes[0,1]); sns.boxplot(x='category', y='value', data=df, ax=axes[1,0]); sns.heatmap(df.corr(), annot=True, ax=axes[1,1]); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()."}]}, {"id": "MCA37510B", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA37510B", "title": "Advance Web Development", "type": "Elective Major Theory + Lab", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA37510B_Advance_Web_Development.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "What will be the output of the following JavaScript snippet? `int a=1; if(a!=null) return 1; else return 0;`", "options": ["1", "0", "compiler error / syntax error", "runtime error"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "`int` is not a valid variable declaration keyword in JavaScript (var, let, const are used), causing a syntax/compiler error."}, {"q": "Which of the following tag is used to embed CSS in an HTML page?", "options": ["<css>", "<!DOCTYPE html>", "<style>", "<script>"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "The `<style>` tag defines internal CSS within an HTML document."}, {"q": "A JavaScript object literal is a list of ________.", "options": ["value:name pairs inside curly braces {}", "name:value pairs inside curly braces {}", "name:value pairs inside square brackets []", "value:name pairs inside square brackets []"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Object literals are defined as comma-separated name:value (or key:value) pairs enclosed in curly braces `{}`."}, {"q": "Bootstrap was initially developed by which organization?", "options": ["Facebook", "Twitter", "Meta", "Yahoo"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Bootstrap was originally created by Mark Otto and Jacob Thornton at Twitter in 2011."}, {"q": "How can you pass dynamic values in JSX?", "options": ["Using props only", "Using the state property only", "Using JavaScript expressions within curly braces {}", "Using plain text"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "In JSX, dynamic JavaScript variables and expressions are embedded using single curly braces `{expression}`."}, {"q": "How does a controlled component differ from an uncontrolled component in React?", "options": ["Controlled components use refs", "Controlled components rely on state for input values", "Uncontrolled components don't use JSX", "Uncontrolled components store data in memory only"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Controlled components store their form data in React state and mutate it via `onChange`, whereas uncontrolled components let the DOM handle state via `ref`."}, {"q": "In React, what is a 'component'?", "options": ["A function or class that takes props and returns UI elements", "A method used exclusively for side effects", "A database connector", "A system for server routing"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Components are independent, reusable building blocks of a React application that accept input (props) and return JSX describing UI."}, {"q": "Variables defined with `const` in JavaScript cannot be ________.", "options": ["Redeclared", "Reassigned", "Incremented", "All of these"], "ans": "(D)", "expl": "`const` variables have block scope and cannot be redeclared, reassigned, or mutated through increment operations."}, {"q": "What happens if `event.preventDefault()` is NOT called in a React form submission handler?", "options": ["The form input will fail", "The form will not be visible", "The page will reload on submit (default browser behavior)", "The component won't render"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "The browser's default action upon form submission is to make an HTTP request and reload the page; `preventDefault()` suppresses this."}, {"q": "What is the full form of JSX in React?", "options": ["JavaScript XTL", "JavaSource XML", "JavaScript XPL", "JavaScript XML"], "ans": "(D)", "expl": "JSX stands for JavaScript XML, a syntax extension for JavaScript allowing HTML-like syntax inside scripts."}, {"q": "What is the advantage of Arrow Functions for event handling in React?", "options": ["They avoid manual binding of 'this' by preserving lexical context", "They improve Bootstrap integration", "They remove the need for CSS", "They create global variables automatically"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Arrow functions capture the `this` value of the enclosing lexical scope, eliminating the need for `this.handleClick = this.handleClick.bind(this)`."}, {"q": "What is the Virtual DOM in React?", "options": ["A DOM management system", "A lightweight in-memory virtual representation of the actual DOM", "A server-side database", "A method to bypass the browser DOM entirely"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Virtual DOM is an in-memory tree representation of the UI synced with the real DOM via the reconciliation diffing algorithm."}, {"q": "Which React hook is used to manage and update state in functional components?", "options": ["state.update()", "useState()", "setState()", "changeState()"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "`useState(initialState)` declares a state variable and provides a setter function to update it in function components."}, {"q": "Which HTTP method is utilized to fetch data from a REST API endpoint in React?", "options": ["POST", "GET", "DELETE", "PATCH"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "The GET method is used for retrieving resource representations without modifying server-side state."}, {"q": "Which command starts the development server in a standard Create React App project?", "options": ["npm run start", "php server start", "node react", "start dev-server"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "`npm start` (or `npm run start`) runs the React development server with hot module reloading at `http://localhost:3000`."}], "group_b": [{"q": "What is JSX in React and how does it differ from traditional HTML?", "marks": 3, "hint": "JSX stands for JavaScript XML. Allows writing HTML-like code inside JS. Differences: uses `className` instead of `class`, `htmlFor` instead of `for`, camelCase event names (`onClick`), and curly braces `{}` to embed expressions."}, {"q": "Differentiate between controlled and uncontrolled components in React with examples.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Controlled: form element's value is bound to React state via `value={state}` and updated using `onChange`. Uncontrolled: form element's value is handled directly by the browser DOM and accessed via `useRef()`."}, {"q": "Explain the advantages of using components in React.js.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Components provide reusability, modularity, separation of concerns, easier unit testing, predictable data flow via props, and maintainable codebase."}, {"q": "How does Bootstrap's responsive 12-column grid system work? Explain `.col-md-6`.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Divides a row into 12 equal vertical columns. `.col-md-6` indicates the column will occupy 6 out of 12 columns (50% viewport width) on medium screens (>=768px) and stack 100% on smaller screens."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of the `useState` hook in React? Provide a code snippet.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Allows adding state to functional components. Syntax: `const [count, setCount] = useState(0);`. Calling `setCount(newVal)` triggers component re-render with updated state."}, {"q": "Explain how React Router enables Single Page Application (SPA) behavior without full page reloads.", "marks": 3, "hint": "React Router intercepts browser URL changes using the HTML5 History API (`pushState`/`popState`) and conditionally mounts matching component views dynamically without refreshing the page."}, {"q": "Why is `event.preventDefault()` crucial in React form submit handlers?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Prevents default browser HTML form submission which triggers a page reload and wipes client-side state. Allows developers to asynchronously dispatch API calls via `fetch` or `axios`."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Design and build an interactive Login Form in React using controlled components. Implement username and password validation, error display, and form submit handling using `event.preventDefault()`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "State object `{username: '', password: ''}` and `{errors: {}}`. In `handleSubmit`: validate username length >= 3 and password >= 6. Show inline red error messages. On valid input, trigger simulated login or alert."}, {"q": "Explain the complete cycle of connecting a React frontend with a PHP & MySQL backend API. Write code for: 1. React `fetch()` POST request, 2. Backend PHP script reading `php://input` and inserting into MySQL, and 3. Returning JSON response with status codes.", "marks": 5, "hint": "React: `fetch('api.php', {method: 'POST', headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}, body: JSON.stringify(data)})`. PHP: `header('Content-Type: application/json'); $data = json_decode(file_get_contents('php://input'), true);` PDO/MySQLi query -> `echo json_encode(['status' => 'success']);`."}, {"q": "Compare CSS Modules, Inline Styling, and Styled Components in React. Evaluate their strengths and weaknesses regarding component encapsulation, dynamic theming via props, and build-time optimization.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Inline styles: JS objects, no media queries/pseudo-selectors, dynamic props. CSS Modules: locally scoped class names via hashing, standard CSS syntax, build-time compilation. Styled-components: CSS-in-JS, full CSS power, tagged template literals, dynamic styles directly using props."}, {"q": "Explain the Virtual DOM and Reconciliation algorithm in React. Detail how React calculates UI diffs and batch updates the real DOM efficiently.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Virtual DOM is a lightweight JS object tree. Reconciliation: when state changes, React creates a new VDOM tree, computes diffs using the heuristic diffing algorithm (O(n) complexity based on component types and keys), and applies minimum batch updates to the real DOM."}, {"q": "Build a React component using React Router (v6) containing a Navigation Bar and three routes: `/home`, `/about`, and `/contact`. Explain the roles of `<BrowserRouter>`, `<Routes>`, `<Route>`, and `<Link>`.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Wrap app with `<BrowserRouter>`. Define navigation using `<Link to='/'>`. Define routes with `<Routes><Route path='/home' element={<Home />} />...</Routes>`. Explain how `<Link>` prevents page refreshes unlike `<a href>`."}, {"q": "Discuss the impact of Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) when making API requests from a React client (e.g. `http://localhost:3000`) to a PHP backend (e.g. `http://localhost:8000`). How do you configure CORS headers in PHP?", "marks": 5, "hint": "Same-Origin Policy blocks cross-origin fetch. Browser sends preflight OPTIONS request. Fix in PHP: `header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *'); header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, OPTIONS'); header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization'); if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] == 'OPTIONS') exit;`."}, {"q": "Analyze the differences between class components and function components with Hooks. Explain how `useState` and `useEffect` replace class lifecycle methods (`componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate`, `componentWillUnmount`).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Function components are simpler, avoid `this` confusion, and promote logic reuse via custom hooks. `useEffect(() => {}, [])` = componentDidMount. `useEffect(() => {}, [dep])` = componentDidUpdate. Cleanup function in `useEffect` = componentWillUnmount."}, {"q": "Create a step-by-step production deployment guide for a React application. Explain the purpose of `npm run build`, the contents of the generated `build/` directory, and how to deploy it on cloud platforms like Vercel or Netlify.", "marks": 5, "hint": "`npm run build` bundles, minifies, and tree-shakes JS/CSS assets via Webpack/Vite into static assets in `build/` or `dist/`. Deploy to Vercel/Netlify: connect GitHub repository, set build command `npm run build` and publish directory `build/`. Configure rewrite rule to route all paths to `index.html` for client-side routing."}]}, {"id": "MCA30511B", "programme": "Master of Computer Applications (MCA) - 2026", "code": "MCA30511B", "title": "AIoT (Artificial Intelligence of Things)", "type": "Elective Major Theory", "fm": 60, "filename": "MCA30511B_AIoT.pdf", "group_a": [{"q": "Artificial Intelligence (AI) primarily aims to:", "options": ["Increase hardware cost", "Enable machines to perform intelligent tasks", "Replace computer networks", "Improve database size"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "AI focuses on creating systems capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence, such as reasoning, learning, and perception."}, {"q": "An AI agent is formally defined as:", "options": ["A programming language", "A software entity that perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it through actuators", "A database server", "A network switch"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "An agent is anything that can perceive its environment through sensors and act upon that environment through actuators."}, {"q": "A rational AI agent always strives to:", "options": ["Minimize performance", "Maximize its expected performance measure based on percept sequence and built-in knowledge", "Reduce memory usage to zero", "Increase program file size"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Rationality in AI means selecting actions expected to maximize the performance measure given available percept history."}, {"q": "Which AI concept is utilized for decision-making in partially observable and uncertain environments?", "options": ["Stack", "Queue", "POMDP (Partially Observable Markov Decision Process)", "Binary Heap"], "ans": "(C)", "expl": "POMDP generalizes MDPs to scenarios where the agent cannot directly observe the complete underlying state."}, {"q": "In a POMDP, what does the 'Belief State' represent?", "options": ["The exact coordinates of the agent", "A probability distribution over all possible states of the world", "Database index records", "Cached network packets"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Because the true state cannot be directly observed in POMDP, the agent maintains a belief state b(s), which is a probability distribution over states."}, {"q": "Which messaging protocol is lightweight, operates on a publish-subscribe architecture over TCP, and is optimized for low-bandwidth IoT sensor nodes?", "options": ["HTTP/1.1", "MQTT (Message Queuing Telemetry Transport)", "FTP", "SNMP"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "MQTT has a minimal 2-byte header, binary payload support, and publish/subscribe architecture, ideal for resource-constrained IoT links."}, {"q": "CoAP stands for:", "options": ["Constrained Application Protocol", "Common Access Protocol", "Cloud Application Program", "Connected API Protocol"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "CoAP stands for Constrained Application Protocol, designed for constrained IoT nodes and M2M communication over UDP."}, {"q": "Edge computing processes data:", "options": ["Near the data source / edge devices", "Only in hyperscale cloud data centers", "At centralized database servers", "Exclusively in client web browsers"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Edge computing brings computation and data storage closer to the devices where data is gathered, minimizing latency and bandwidth usage."}, {"q": "Which neural network architecture is specifically designed for processing sequential and time-series data?", "options": ["CNN", "RNN / LSTM", "DBSCAN", "K-Means"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks are designed to process temporal and sequential data streams."}, {"q": "In IoT systems, what is the function of an Actuator?", "options": ["To collect physical signals from the environment", "To perform physical actions or control mechanisms in response to electrical commands", "To store big data files", "To assign IP addresses to WiFi chips"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "Sensors perceive the physical world; actuators convert electrical control signals into physical actions (valves, motors, relays)."}, {"q": "Which cloud platform is specifically referenced for IoT device registry, shadow management, and data ingestion?", "options": ["AWS IoT", "Adobe Photoshop", "Microsoft Word", "Oracle Forms"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "AWS IoT Core provides managed cloud services to connect, manage, and process messages from billions of IoT devices."}, {"q": "In Industry 4.0, what does 'Predictive Maintenance' accomplish?", "options": ["Detects and forecasts equipment degradation and failures before they occur using sensor analytics", "Reduces RAM allocation", "Increases monitor refresh rate", "Deletes operational log files"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "Predictive maintenance leverages continuous sensor data (vibration, temperature) and ML to predict mechanical failures before they cause downtime."}, {"q": "What communication model allows two IoT devices to exchange data directly without passing through a cloud intermediary?", "options": ["Device-to-Device (D2D)", "Device-to-Cloud", "Client-to-Server", "Machine-to-Human"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "D2D communication establishes direct peer-to-peer communication between proximate devices using protocols like BLE, Zigbee, or WiFi Direct."}, {"q": "Which clustering algorithm can discover clusters of arbitrary shapes and distinguish noise/outliers?", "options": ["K-Means", "DBSCAN", "Linear Regression", "Decision Tree"], "ans": "(B)", "expl": "DBSCAN is a density-based spatial clustering algorithm capable of finding arbitrarily shaped clusters and flagging outliers as noise."}, {"q": "AIoT fundamentally represents the fusion of:", "options": ["Artificial Intelligence and Internet of Things", "AI and Relational Databases", "IoT and Printers", "Cloud and Browsers"], "ans": "(A)", "expl": "AIoT is the integration of Artificial Intelligence infrastructure and machine learning capabilities with the Internet of Things network."}], "group_b": [{"q": "Define Artificial Intelligence (AI) and explain the concept of an AI Agent.", "marks": 3, "hint": "AI enables machines to simulate human intelligence. An AI agent is a software or hardware entity that perceives its environment through sensors, processes information, and acts upon the environment using actuators to achieve specific goals."}, {"q": "What is a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP)? Define Belief State.", "marks": 3, "hint": "POMDP models decision making when an agent cannot directly observe current state. Belief state b(s) is a probability distribution over all possible states of the world updated upon receiving new actions and observations."}, {"q": "What is a Multi-Agent System (MAS)? List two advantages of Multi-Agent Systems in IoT.", "marks": 3, "hint": "A system composed of multiple interacting intelligent agents. Advantages: 1. Distributed problem solving and fault tolerance (no single point of failure), 2. Scalability and parallel task execution across distributed IoT nodes."}, {"q": "Differentiate between MQTT and CoAP protocols in IoT communication.", "marks": 3, "hint": "MQTT: TCP-based, publish-subscribe architecture with central broker, persistent connections, 2-byte header. CoAP: UDP-based, client-server RESTful (GET/POST) architecture, asynchronous observe, lightweight 4-byte header for constrained nodes."}, {"q": "Explain the difference between Edge Computing and Fog Computing in AIoT architectures.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Edge: computation performed directly on or adjacent to sensor devices (microcontrollers, smartphones). Fog: computing layer located on local area network gateways and local servers between edge devices and the central cloud."}, {"q": "What is Predictive Maintenance in Industry 4.0? How does it differ from Preventive Maintenance?", "marks": 3, "hint": "Preventive maintenance replaces parts on fixed schedules regardless of condition. Predictive maintenance uses real-time AIoT sensor telemetry (vibration, heat) to monitor equipment health and forecast failures before they occur."}, {"q": "Compare supervised learning and unsupervised learning with practical AIoT examples.", "marks": 3, "hint": "Supervised: learns from labeled datasets (e.g. classification of healthy vs defective motors in factory). Unsupervised: discovers hidden patterns in unlabeled data (e.g. DBSCAN clustering of anomalous network sensor traffic)."}], "group_c": [{"q": "Explain the layered architecture of the Internet of Things (IoT) with a neat diagram. Detail the Perception Layer, Network Layer, Edge/Fog Layer, and Cloud Application Layer.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Diagram showing 4 layers: 1. Perception (sensors, actuators, RFID), 2. Network (WiFi, BLE, LoRaWAN, 5G), 3. Edge/Fog (local gateways, filtering, real-time inference), 4. Cloud Application (AWS IoT, big data storage, analytics dashboards)."}, {"q": "Explain the architecture and working mechanism of Multi-Agent Systems (MAS). Discuss how cooperative AI agents collaborate to solve complex distributed real-world challenges.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Agents possess autonomy, local views, and decentralized control. Interaction via Agent Communication Languages (ACL/FIPA). Contract Net Protocol for task announcement, bidding, and award. Applications: smart grid power distribution, autonomous swarm robotics."}, {"q": "Discuss Deep Learning architectures for AIoT: Compare Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks based on architecture, data types, and IoT applications.", "marks": 5, "hint": "CNN: spatial feature extraction via convolution filters and pooling; used for edge camera vision and acoustic spectrograms. LSTM: recurrent cell with input, forget, and output gates maintaining long-term dependencies; used for time-series vibration and sensor anomaly detection."}, {"q": "Explain the communication models in IoT: Machine-to-Machine (M2M), Device-to-Device (D2D), and Device-to-Cloud. Detail the role of IP addressing and network routing protocols (RPL, 6LoWPAN).", "marks": 5, "hint": "M2M: point-to-point automated communication. D2D: proximate direct wireless exchange. Device-to-Cloud: ingestion via MQTT/HTTPS to platforms like AWS IoT. Detail 6LoWPAN header compression allowing IPv6 packets over IEEE 802.15.4."}, {"q": "Design an AIoT-based Smart Healthcare Patient Monitoring System. Detail sensor selection (ECG, SpO2, temperature), edge gateway microcontroller, wireless transmission, real-time anomaly detection, and cloud medical dashboard.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Wearable sensors -> BLE -> ESP32/Raspberry Pi Edge Gateway -> 1D-CNN arrhythmia classifier -> MQTTS encrypted dispatch to cloud -> Push alerts to hospital doctors and historical EHR storage."}, {"q": "Analyze Security and Privacy Challenges in AIoT systems. Discuss security threats (Mirai botnet, eavesdropping, sensor spoofing) and mitigation mechanisms (mutual TLS authentication, AES encryption, and secure boot).", "marks": 5, "hint": "Constrained hardware limits crypto operations. Threat vectors: default passwords, unsecured MQTT topics. Hardening: hardware root of trust, secure boot, X.509 device certificates for mTLS over port 8883, AES-128 hardware acceleration."}, {"q": "Explain the architecture and core services of AWS IoT Core. Detail Device Gateway, Message Broker, Rules Engine, Device Shadow, and Device Registry.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Device Gateway: terminates secure TLS connections. Message Broker: scalable publish/subscribe MQTT engine. Rules Engine: SQL-like syntax routing messages to Lambda, S3, or DynamoDB. Device Shadow: virtual JSON document storing device state even when offline."}, {"q": "Discuss AIoT applications in Industry 4.0. Present a comprehensive Case Study on Predictive Maintenance and Anomaly Detection in manufacturing machines using Edge AI.", "marks": 5, "hint": "Case study: industrial CNC milling motor. Accelerometer samples at 10kHz. TinyML autoencoder runs on edge microcontroller. High reconstruction error indicates bearing wear. Prevents unexpected assembly line shutdowns and saves millions in repair costs."}]}];
